messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nThe reason why a large number of spitters spit at will and without scruples is that the previous punishment measures are far from being a warning and it is more difficult to touch the heart of the spitters. They had no sense of shame when they spited and no shame after being punished.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following options does the author think is the best way to punish people who spit everywhere?\n# Options:\nA) We should give them proper guidance.\nB) Touch the depths of their heart.\nC) Not specified.\nD) We need to specify the punishment measures.. I think the correct option is: B\n', '<think>Okay, let\'s tackle this problem. So the question is about figuring out which punishment method the author thinks is best for people who spit everywhere. The context given says that the reason many people spit without any qualms is because previous punishment measures aren\'t effective as warnings and it\'s hard to touch the "heart" of the spitters. Also, they don\'t feel shame when they do it or even after being punished.\n\nHmm, the key points here are that current punishments aren\'t serving as good warnings and don\'t reach the spitters\' hearts. The problem is that the existing measures aren\'t making the offenders feel ashamed. The author is pointing out that the punishments are insufficient in two ways: they don\'t deter others (not a warning) and they don\'t affect the spitters\' conscience (don\'t touch their heart). .\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nA) Proper guidance. The context mentions punishment measures, so maybe guidance isn\'t the same as punishment. The author is talking about punishment effectiveness, not education.\n\nB) Touch the depths of their heart. The original text says the previous measures "more difficult to touch the heart of the spitters." So the author is implying that the current punishments don\'t do this, so maybe the solution is to do exactly that—make sure the punishment touches their heart, leading to shame.\n\nC) Not specified. Well, the question is asking what the author thinks, so maybe the answer is in the text. Since the author mentions that touching the heart is a problem, the solution would logically be to address that, so C might not be right.\n\nD) Specify the punishment measures. The text says previous measures were not effective. Maybe the author suggests making the punishments more specific? But the main issue was about not touching the heart, not the specificity. The first part says the punishment is "far from being a warning," which could mean they aren\'t strict enough or not enforced. But the second part is about not touching the heart. .\n\nWait, the question is asking for the author\'s suggested best way. The problem is that current punishments don\'t work as warnings and don\'t touch the heart. The author\'s reasoning is that the spitters have no shame, so the punishment needs to address that lack of shame. The key part is "it is more difficult to touch the heart of the spitters." So the author is saying that the punishment should be such that it does touch their heart, making them feel ashamed. Therefore, the answer would be B. .\n\nOption D talks about specifying the punishment measures, but the text doesn\'t mention that the measures are unspecified, just that they\'re ineffective in warning and touching the heart. So maybe B is the answer. Let me check again.\n\nThe original context: the reason for spitting is because previous punishments aren\'t a good warning and don\'t touch their hearts. The author is pointing out that since the punishments don\'t touch their hearts, leading to no shame, the solution must involve touching their hearts. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
