dopsis\". So the research was done on Arabidopsis. The conclusion is about applying this to vegetables. The missing link is that the same effect occurs in vegetables. Therefore, B is necessary. So the correct answer is B.\n\nHmm. So the initial thought of D is wrong, but why did I think that? Maybe getting confused with practical considerations, but the question is about logical validity. Therefore, B must be the answer.\n</think>\n\nThe researcher's conclusion that applying this transgenic method to vegetables can reduce pesticide use relies on the assumption that the mechanism tested in Arabidopsis (enhanced fragrance attracting parasitoids when injured) will also work in actual vegetable crops. This requires the premise that **the transgenic vegetables will exhibit the same response**\u2014enhanced fragrance after injury. Without this, the conclusion lacks validity, as the study's success in Arabidopsis does not guarantee it applies to other vegetables.\n\nOption **B** directly addresses this gap by asserting that the fragrance of transgenic vegetable leaves will be enhanced after injury, ensuring the mechanism works in the target crops. Other options are either redundant (e.g., the context implies pesticides target pests) or irrelevant to logical validity (e.g., legal considerations in D do not affect the argument's internal logic).\n\n**Answer: B**",
