en by the CEO. To weaken this, we need evidence that HRM has a more influential or strategic role than claimed.\n\n**Analysis of Options:**\n- **A)** HR employees' experience in top companies does not address their role in decision-making. It strengthens their competence but not their strategic involvement.\n- **B)** Designing a compensation system to retain talent is part of the traditional support role. This aligns with the argument rather than weakening it.\n- **C)** If an HR manager participates in top decision-making meetings, it directly contradicts the premise that HRM is excluded from strategic decisions. This undermines the conclusion that HRM is purely supportive.\n- **D)** Hiring middle managers is within HRM\u2019s conventional responsibilities and does not challenge the argument about strategic exclusion.\n\n**Conclusion:** **C** weakens the argument most effectively by demonstrating that HRM can have a strategic role in decision-making, countering the claim that it is limited to support. \n\n**Answer:** C",
