sub-committees**, while the others are in one each.\n\n3. **Evaluating the Options**:\n   - **Option A**: \"One of F or G is a member of three sub-committees.\"  \n     *Counterexample*: If the triple-member is H, neither F nor G is in all three. This is possible, so **A is not necessarily true**.\n\n   - **Option B**: \"One of H or I is a member of three sub-committees.\"  \n     *Counterexample*: If the triple-member is M (as shown in the analysis), neither H nor I is in all three. **B is not necessarily true**.\n\n   - **Option C**: \"P or M is a member of only one sub-committee.\"  \n     *Counterexample*: If the triple-member is P, then P is in all three, and M could be in two sub-committees (e.g., Sub1 and Sub3), leaving neither P nor M in only one. Thus, **C is not necessarily true**.\n\n   - **Option D**: \"One member happens to be a member of two sub-committees.\"  \n     *Mathematical Necessity*: The remaining five members must fill \\(6\\) slots after accounting for the triple-member. Since \\(5\\) members in one sub-committee each would only fill \\(5\\) slots, **one must be in two sub-committees**. This is unavoidable. **D must be true**.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe only option that must hold under all valid configurations is **D**.\n\n**Answer: D**",
