messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nA committee works for two years and is composed of 4 members each year, 2 of which are from the following 4 judges: F, G, H and I, and the other 2 members are from the following 3 scientists: V, Y and Z. Each year, the committee is chaired by one member. A member who chairs the first year must retire from the committee during the second year. The person who chairs the second year must be a member of the committee during the first year. The committee members must meet the following conditions: G and V cannot be members of the committee in the same year. H and Y cannot be members of this committee in the same year. Each year, only one of I and V is a member of this committee.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, if H is the chairman in the first year, which of the followings can be the chairman in the second year?\n# Options:\nA) F\nB) G\nC) Y\nD) I. I think the correct option is: D\n', '<think>Okay, let me try to work through this problem step by step. So, the question is about a committee that works for two years, with specific composition rules each year. The problem states that H is the chairman in the first year, and we need to figure out who can be the chairman in the second year from the options given. .\n\nFirst, I need to recall all the constraints provided. Let me list them out again to make sure I don\'t miss anything:.\n\n1. Each year, the committee has 4 members: 2 judges from F, G, H, I and 2 scientists from V, Y, Z.\n2. Each year, one member chairs. .\n3. If someone chairs the first year, they must retire in the second year. .\n4. The second year\'s chair must have been a member in the first year.\n5. Constraints:.\n   a) G and V can\'t be in the same year.\n   b) H and Y can\'t be in the same year.\n   c) Each year, only one of I or V is a member. .\n\nGiven that H is the chairman in the first year, so first, I need to figure out the possible members of the first year committee, then see who can be the second year\'s chair.\n\nStarting with the first year:.\n\nSince H is the chairman, they must be a member of the first year\'s committee. Since the committee has 4 members, 2 judges and 2 scientists. Since H is a judge, we need one more judge from F, G, I (since H is already there). Also, the two scientists must be chosen from V, Y, Z, but with some constraints.\n\nBut let\'s not get ahead of ourselves. Let\'s structure this:.\n\nFirst Year Committee (Year 1):.\n\n- Judges: H (since he\'s chair) + 1 more from F, G, I.\n- Scientists: 2 from V, Y, Z, but considering the constraints.\n\nConstraints for Year 1:.\n\n- G and V cannot be together. So if G is a judge, then V can\'t be a scientist that year.\n- H and Y can\'t be together. Since H is already in the committee, Y cannot be a scientist in Year 1.\n- Only one of I or V can be present. Wait, actually, the third condition says "each year, only one of I and V is a member". So in any given year, you can\'t have both I and V. So if I is a judge, then V can\'t be a scientist, and vice versa.\n\nSo let\'s break down the possible first year committees.\n\nFirst, since H is a judge, the other judge must be F, G, or I.\n\nLet me consider each possibility for the second judge:.\n\nCase 1: Second judge is F.\n\nSo Judges: H and F.\n\nScientists: Need 2 from V, Y, Z, but with constraints.\n\nBut H is present, so Y cannot be in the scientists. So scientists can be V and Z, or V and Y? Wait no, H and Y can\'t be together. Since H is there, Y cannot be part of the committee. So scientists can only be V and Z, or V and Y? Wait, Y is excluded because of H\'s presence. So Y is out. So scientists must be two from V and Z. But there are only two scientists needed. So possible combinations are V and Z, or just one of them? Wait, need two scientists. Since there are three scientists: V, Y, Z. But Y is excluded, so only V and Z. Since we need two, it\'s V and Z. .\n\nBut also, we have the constraint that only one of I or V can be in the committee. But in this case, the second judge is F, so I is not in the committee. Therefore, V can be in the scientists. So V is allowed here. So the scientists could be V and Z. .\n\nAdditionally, check if G is present: In this case, the second judge is F, so G isn\'t in the committee. Therefore, the G and V constraint doesn\'t apply here (since G isn\'t there), so that\'s okay. .\n\nSo in this case, possible committee members for Year 1:.\n\nJudges: H, F.\n\nScientists: V, Z.\n\nAlternatively, could scientists be just Z and another? Wait, no, since Y is excluded, so only V and Z. So that\'s the only option here. .\n\nAlternatively, if scientists were, say, V and Z, that\'s okay. .\n\nCase 2: Second judge is G.\n\nJudges: H and G.\n\nScientists: Need two from V, Y, Z, but with constraints.\n\nFirst, H is present, so Y cannot be in the scientists. So scientists can be V and Z, but also need to check the G and V constraint. Since G is a judge here, V cannot be in the scientists. So if G is a judge, then the scientists must be Y and Z? But Y can\'t be because of H. Wait, Y is excluded because of H, so the only options are V and Z. But since G is present, V can\'t be there. Therefore, the scientists must be Z and.. but we need two. Since Y is excluded, and V is excluded due to G, there\'s only Z left. But that\'s only one scientist. That\'s a problem. .\n\nWait, that can\'t work. Because we need two scientists. So if the second judge is G, then scientists can\'t include V or Y, so only Z. But that\'s only one, so that\'s impossible. Therefore, this case is invalid. .\n\nTherefore, if the second judge is G, we can\'t form a valid committee because we can\'t get two scientists. So Case 2 is impossible. .\n\nCase 3: Second judge is I.\n\nJudges: H and I.\n\nScientists: Two from V, Y, Z. .\n\nAgain, H is there, so Y can\'t be included. So scientists are from V and Z. .\n\nNow, the constraint that only one of I or V can be present. Since I is a judge, then V cannot be a scientist. Therefore, the scientists must be Z and.. but again, only Z and V? Wait, since V is excluded, scientists must be Z and someone else. But since Y is excluded, the only other is Z and.. but only two scientists needed, so maybe Z and another? Wait, there\'s only V and Z left (since Y is excluded). Since V is excluded because I is present, then scientists can only be Z and.. but that\'s only one. Wait, that\'s a problem. .\n\nWait, hold on. If the second judge is I, then the scientists can\'t include V (due to the I/V rule). So the scientists have to be two from Y and Z, but H is present so Y is excluded. So only Z is left. Again, we can\'t get two scientists. That\'s a problem. .\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nWait, the scientists are chosen from V, Y, Z. But with H present, Y is excluded. So available scientists are V and Z. But if I is a judge, then V can\'t be in the committee. So scientists have to be Z and.. but only Z is left. So we can\'t have two scientists. Therefore, this case also is impossible. .\n\nSo, the only possible case where the second judge is F. So in Year 1, the committee must be:.\n\nJudges: H and F.\n\nScientists: V and Z.\n\nAlternatively, wait, maybe another combination? Let me see again. .\n\nWait, in Case 1 where the second judge is F:.\n\nJudges H and F.\n\nScientists must be two from V, Y, Z, but Y is excluded (due to H), so V and Z. So that works. .\n\nAlternatively, could scientists be V and Z, which is okay. .\n\nAlternatively, could we have scientists as Z and another? No, since only two. So that\'s the only possibility here. .\n\nWait, but what if the scientists were V and Z, so that\'s okay. .\n\nSo that\'s the only valid first year committee when the second judge is F. .\n\nTherefore, the first year committee is:.\n\nJudges: H, F.\n\nScientists: V, Z.\n\nNow, moving to the second year. .\n\nThe constraints for the second year:.\n\n- The second year\'s chair must have been a member in the first year. .\n\n- The first year\'s chair (H) must retire, so H cannot be in the second year. .\n\nSo, the possible chairs for the second year must be among the first year\'s members except H. .\n\nFirst year\'s members (excluding H) are F, V, Z, and the other judge (F in this case). Wait, let me list all first year members:.\n\nJudges: H, F.\n\nScientists: V, Z.\n\nSo all members except H are F, V, Z. .\n\nWait, the second judge was F, so the members are H, F (judges) and V, Z (scientists). .\n\nTherefore, the possible candidates for second year chair must be among F, V, Z. .\n\nAdditionally, the second year\'s committee must again have 2 judges and 2 scientists, with the chair being one of them. Also, the constraints must hold for the second year as well. .\n\nWait, but the question is asking "which of the following can be the chairman in the second year?" The options are A-F, B-G, C-Y, D-I. .\n\nWait, so looking at the options, the possible chairs are among the first year\'s members except H. So the options given are F, G, Y, I. .\n\nWait, but in the first year\'s members, G was not present (since second judge was F). So G wasn\'t in the first year committee. Therefore, G cannot be the second year\'s chair because the second year chair must have been a member in the first year. So G is out. So option B is invalid. .\n\nSimilarly, Y was not in the first year\'s committee (since H was there, so Y was excluded). So Y also wasn\'t in the first year, so they can\'t be the chair. So option C is out. .\n\nSo possible chairs are F or I, but I wasn\'t in the first year committee either. Wait, in the first year committee, the judges were H and F. So I wasn\'t there. So I can\'t be the second year chair. Wait, but option D is I. Hmm. Wait, so maybe I need to check other possibilities. .\n\nWait, perhaps there\'s another first year committee composition? Because I assumed the second judge was F, but maybe there\'s another way. Wait, let me check again. .\n\nWait, in case 3, when second judge is I, we had a problem with not enough scientists, but maybe I made a mistake there. Let me re-examine that. .\n\nCase 3: Second judge is I. .\n\nJudges: H and I. .\n\nScientists: Need two from V, Y, Z, but H is present so Y is excluded. So scientists can be V and Z. .\n\nBut the constraint says only one of I or V can be in the committee. Since I is a judge, V cannot be in the scientists. Therefore, scientists can\'t include V. So the scientists must be Z and.. but Y is excluded, so only Z is left. Not enough. .\n\nWait, so that\'s impossible. .\n\nWait another thought: Wait, maybe the scientists can choose Z and another? But there\'s only two options: V and Z. Since V is excluded, only Z. So that\'s not possible. .\n\nHmm. So that case is invalid. .\n\nCase 2 with second judge G also failed. .\n\nTherefore the only possible first year committee is with judges H and F, scientists V and Z. .\n\nTherefore, the first year members are H, F, V, Z. .\n\nTherefore, second year chair must be one of F, V, or Z. .\n\nLooking at the options, the possible chairs are F (option A), or maybe someone else. Wait the options given are A-F, B-G, C-Y, D-I. .\n\nSo among the options, only F is possible, since the others (G, Y, I) were not in the first year. .\n\nWait, but wait the problem says "the person who chairs the second year must be a member of the committee during the first year." So only F, V, or Z can be the second chair. .\n\nTherefore, the options given include F (A), but the other options (B-G, C-Y, D-I) weren\'t in the first year. .\n\nBut the question is asking "which of the following can be the chairman in the second year?" So the answer would be A) F. .\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me think again. Because maybe there are other possible first year committees that I didn\'t consider. .\n\nWait, perhaps I missed another possible first year committee? Let me think again. .\n\nWait, in the first case where the second judge is F, the scientists are V and Z. But perhaps there\'s another combination? For example, scientists could be Z and another, but no, since Y is excluded. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the scientists are V and Z, which is the only option. .\n\nAlternatively, could the scientists be V and Z, so that\'s okay. .\n\nAlternatively, what if the second judge was F, but the scientists are V and Z. .\n\nWait, but what if the second judge was F, but instead of V, could they have another scientist? Wait, no, because if the second judge is F, then I is not in the committee, so the I/V rule allows V to be present. But H is there so Y can\'t be. So only V and Z are available. .\n\nTherefore, that\'s the only possible first year committee. .\n\nTherefore, the second year chair must be F, V, or Z. .\n\nLooking at the options given, only F is listed (option A). .\n\nWait, but the options also include I (option D). How could I be considered? .\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in assuming the second judge had to be F. Let me see again. .\n\nWait, is there another way to form the first year committee? Let me think again. .\n\nWait, the second judge could also be someone else, but earlier analysis suggested that only F works. Let me re-examine the second judge possibilities again. .\n\nWait, let\'s see: .\n\nSuppose the second judge is I. .\n\nThen judges are H and I. .\n\nScientists must be two from V, Y, Z. But H is present, so Y can\'t be there. .\n\nScientists can be V and Z, but then the I/V rule says only one of I or V can be present. Since I is in the committee (as a judge), V can\'t be in the scientists. .\n\nTherefore, scientists have to be Z and.. but there\'s only Z left (since V is excluded and Y is excluded). Not enough. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe scientists could be Z and another? But there\'s no other available. .\n\nHmm. So that\'s impossible. .\n\nCase 2: second judge G. .\n\nJudges H and G. .\n\nScientists need two from V, Y, Z. But H is there, so Y can\'t be. Also, G is present, so V can\'t be there. .\n\nTherefore, scientists must be Z and.. but again, only Z is left. Not enough. .\n\nSo only Case 1 works. .\n\nTherefore, the first year must have judges H and F, scientists V and Z. .\n\nTherefore, the possible chairs for the second year are F, V, or Z. .\n\nLooking at the options, only F is listed (option A). .\n\nWait, but the options include I (D). How could I be a possible chair? .\n\nAh, maybe there\'s another scenario where the first year committee includes I? .\n\nWait, but according to the constraints, if the first year\'s second judge is I, then the scientists can\'t have V. Let me think again. .\n\nWait, perhaps the scientists could be Y and Z? Wait no, because H is present, so Y can\'t be there. .\n\nWait, maybe I made a mistake in the constraints. Let me check again. .\n\nThe constraints are:.\n\n- G and V can\'t be in the same year.\n\n- H and Y can\'t be in the same year.\n\n- Only one of I or V can be present each year. .\n\nSo in the case where the second judge is I (so judges H and I), scientists can\'t have V (because I is present) and can\'t have Y (because H is present). Therefore, scientists must be Z and.. but only Z is available. So that\'s impossible. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a different configuration where the first year has different members. .\n\nWait, perhaps the second judge is F, but the scientists are Z and someone else? Wait, but Y is excluded, so only V and Z. .\n\nWait, maybe the scientists are V and Z. .\n\nSo the first year committee is H, F, V, Z. .\n\nTherefore, the second year\'s chair must be among F, V, or Z. .\n\nSo the options given are A-F, B-G, C-Y, D-I. .\n\nTherefore, the answer must be A. .\n\nBut the options also have D) I. How can I be considered? .\n\nWait, perhaps I\'m missing another possible first year committee where I is a member. Let me think again. .\n\nWait, maybe if the second judge is I, but somehow the scientists can have two people without violating the constraints. .\n\nWait, let me try again. .\n\nSuppose second judge is I. .\n\nJudges: H and I. .\n\nScientists must be two from V, Y, Z. .\n\nConstraints:.\n\n- H is there, so Y can\'t be. .\n\n- I is there, so V can\'t be. .\n\nThus, scientists must be Z and.. but only Z is available. Not possible. .\n\nSo that\'s impossible. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the first year has different judges? .\n\nWait, what if the second judge is F, but the scientists are Z and another? Wait, no, only V and Z. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the scientists are V and Z. .\n\nWait, that\'s the only possibility. .\n\nTherefore, the first year committee is fixed as H, F, V, Z. .\n\nThus, the second year chair can only be F, V, or Z. .\n\nNone of the options except F (A) are in that list. .\n\nWait, but the problem\'s options include D) I. Maybe I\'m missing something here. .\n\nWait, perhaps the second year committee can include I, but only if they weren\'t in the first year. Wait, but to be the chair, they must have been in the first year. Since I wasn\'t in the first year, they can\'t be the chair. .\n\nWait, but maybe there\'s another way to form the first year committee where I was a member. Let me see. .\n\nWait, what if the second judge is I, but somehow scientists can be Y and Z? .\n\nWait, but H is present, so Y can\'t be there. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe I is a judge and scientists are V and Z? But then V and I can\'t be together. .\n\nWait, so V is excluded if I is present. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I made a mistake in the constraints. Let me recheck the third constraint: "Each year, only one of I and V is a member of this committee." .\n\nAh, that means in any year, you can\'t have both I and V. So if I is present as a judge, then V can\'t be a scientist. .\n\nSo if in the first year, if I is a judge, then V can\'t be a scientist. But then the scientists would need to be two from Y and Z, but Y can\'t be because of H. So only Z is left. .\n\nThus, impossible. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nWait, perhaps the second judge is F, but the scientists are Z and another? No, only V and Z. .\n\nWait, unless the scientists are Z and.. maybe another scientist? Wait, there are three scientists: V, Y, Z. But Y is excluded because of H. .\n\nSo only V and Z. .\n\nTherefore, the first year committee must be H, F, V, Z. .\n\nTherefore, the second year chair must be F, V, or Z. .\n\nThe options given are A-F, so that\'s the answer. .\n\nWait, but the problem is presented as a multiple-choice question where the options are A-F, B-G, C-Y, D-I. .\n\nBut according to this, the answer should be A. .\n\nHowever, maybe I made a mistake in assuming that the second judge must be F. Let me think again. .\n\nWait, perhaps the first year committee could have judges H and G, but then the scientists would have to be.. .\n\nWait, let\'s try again for case 2. .\n\nCase 2: second judge is G. .\n\nJudges: H and G. .\n\nScientists: two from V, Y, Z. .\n\nConstraints:.\n\n- H is present, so Y can\'t be. .\n\n- G is present, so V can\'t be. .\n\nSo scientists must be two from Z (since Y and V are excluded). But there\'s only Z left. So that\'s impossible. .\n\nThus, invalid. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nWait, is there any other way? .\n\nWait, perhaps the first year\'s scientists include Y and Z? .\n\nWait, but H is present, so Y can\'t be there. .\n\nNo. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake with the judge selection. .\n\nWait, the committee has 2 judges each year from F, G, H, I. .\n\nIf the first year has H and F as judges, then the scientists must be V and Z. .\n\nAlternatively, could the scientists be V and Z, which is okay. .\n\nThus, the first year is fixed. .\n\nTherefore, the second year chair can only be F, V, or Z. .\n\nLooking at the options, only F is present (option A). .\n\nTherefore, the answer should be A. .\n\nBut let me check the options again to make sure. .\n\nOptions:.\n\nA) F.\n\nB) G.\n\nC) Y.\n\nD) I.\n\nSo, yes, A is an option. .\n\nWait, but the problem says "the committee members must meet the following conditions: G and V cannot be members of the committee in the same year. H and Y cannot be members of this committee in the same year. Each year, only one of I and V is a member of this committee.".\n\nWait, so in the first year, with H and F as judges, and V and Z as scientists, that\'s okay because:.\n\n- G is not present, so G and V not together is okay.\n\n- H and Y not together (Y isn\'t there).\n\n- Only one of I or V: V is present, and I is not, so okay. .\n\nYes, that\'s valid. .\n\nTherefore, second year chair must be one of F, V, or Z. .\n\nSo the possible options are A. .\n\nWait, but the problem says "can be the chairman in the second year". So maybe there are other possibilities where the first year committee is different? .\n\nWait, perhaps there is another valid first year committee where I is a member, which would allow I to be a possible chair? .\n\nWait, let me try again to see if there\'s another way. .\n\nSuppose the second judge is I. .\n\nJudges: H and I. .\n\nScientists: Need two from V, Y, Z. .\n\nBut H is there, so Y is excluded. .\n\nScientists must be two from V and Z. .\n\nHowever, since I is a judge, V cannot be in the scientists (due to the I/V rule). .\n\nSo scientists must be Z and.. but only Z is available. .\n\nThus, impossible. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the first year has judges H and G, but scientists are.. .\n\nWait, no, as before. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the first year has judges H and F, scientists Z and Y? .\n\nWait, but H is present so Y cannot be there. .\n\nNo. .\n\nAlternatively, scientists Z and someone else? No, only V and Z. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the first year committee has judges H and F, scientists V and Z. .\n\nThen in the second year, the chair can be F, V, or Z. .\n\nSo, for example, if the second year chair is F, that\'s allowed. .\n\nAlternatively, could the second year\'s chair be V? .\n\nWell, V was a scientist in the first year, so they can be chair in the second year, provided that the second year\'s committee includes them. .\n\nBut the question is only asking who can be the chair, not necessarily that they must be. .\n\nWait, the question is "which of the followings can be the chairman in the second year?" So any of the options that is possible. .\n\nSo, if F is possible, then A is correct. .\n\nBut maybe there\'s another scenario where another option is possible. .\n\nWait, let\'s suppose that in the first year, the second judge is F, and scientists are V and Z. .\n\nNow, for the second year, the chair must be someone from the first year except H. .\n\nSo possible chairs are F, V, Z. .\n\nBut the options given don\'t include V or Z, only F, G, Y, I. .\n\nTherefore, among the options, only F is possible. .\n\nTherefore, the answer must be A) F. .\n\nBut wait, the problem might have a trick. Let me check again. .\n\nWait, perhaps the second judge in the first year could be someone else if we have different scientists. Wait, no. .\n\nWait another thought: Maybe the first year\'s scientists are Z and Y? But no, because H is present so Y can\'t be there. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the first year has different judges? Let me see. .\n\nWait, the second judge must be one of F, G, I. .\n\nWe saw that only F works. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the first year has judges H and G, but scientists are Z and.. but that\'s impossible. .\n\nWait, maybe the problem allows the scientists to choose two from V and Z, but since there are only two, that\'s okay. .\n\nWait, but in the case of second judge being G, scientists can\'t include V (because G is there) or Y (because H is there), so only Z is left. Not enough. .\n\nTherefore, the only possible first year committee is H, F, V, Z. .\n\nThus, the second year\'s chair must be among F, V, or Z. .\n\nSince the options don\'t include V or Z, the answer is A. .\n\nWait, but I\'m a bit confused because the options include I (D). Maybe I missed a case where the first year committee includes I. .\n\nWait, let me try again. .\n\nSuppose the second judge is I, but the scientists are Z and.. .\n\nWait, H and I as judges. Scientists must be two, but can\'t include V (because of I) or Y (because of H). So only Z is available. Not enough. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the second judge is I and the scientists are Z and someone else? .\n\nWait, but there\'s no one else. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the first year committee has judges H and I, and scientists are Z and.. but that\'s impossible. .\n\nTherefore, I can\'t be in the first year\'s committee without making the scientists impossible. .\n\nTherefore, the only possible chair is F (A). .\n\nWait, but let me check the options again. The options are A-F, B-G, C-Y, D-I. .\n\nIs there any possibility that I could be the chair? .\n\nWell, to be the chair in the second year, I must have been a member in the first year. .\n\nTherefore, unless I was in the first year\'s committee, he can\'t be the chair. .\n\nBut in the first year\'s committee, we can\'t have I unless we can form a valid committee with him. .\n\nWhich we can\'t, as shown earlier. .\n\nTherefore, I can\'t be in the first year\'s committee, so can\'t be chair. .\n\nThus, the answer is A. .\n\nWait, but maybe I made a mistake in assuming that the second judge must be F. What if there\'s another configuration where the first year committee includes I? .\n\nWait, let me think differently. .\n\nSuppose in the first year, the judges are H and I. .\n\nScientists must be two from V, Y, Z. .\n\nBut H is there, so Y is out. .\n\nI is there, so V is out. .\n\nThus, scientists must be Z and.. but only Z. Not enough. .\n\nThus, impossible. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, if the first year\'s judges are H and G, then scientists can\'t have V or Y, so only Z. Not enough. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nWait, perhaps the first year committee has judges H and F, scientists V and Z. .\n\nThen, in the second year, the committee must have 4 members again, with 2 judges and 2 scientists. .\n\nThe second year\'s chair must be someone from the first year\'s members except H. .\n\nSo possible chairs are F, V, Z. .\n\nNow, for the second year, the chair could be, say, V. .\n\nIs that possible? .\n\nLet\'s see. .\n\nSecond year\'s committee must have:.\n\n- 2 judges, 2 scientists. .\n\n- The chair (say V) must be a member. .\n\nBut V is a scientist, so the chair can be a scientist. .\n\nBut the constraints must hold for the second year as well. .\n\nLet me think through an example where the second year chair is F (option A). .\n\nSecond year\'s committee could be:.\n\nJudges: F and maybe another judge (could be G, I, or someone else, but must have been in first year or not? Wait, no, the committee members can be anyone except the first year\'s chair (H) and must follow the constraints. .\n\nWait, the second year\'s committee can include new members, but the chair must have been in the first year. .\n\nWait, the committee composition for the second year must have 2 judges from F, G, H, I (but H is retired). So judges available are F, G, I. .\n\nScientists are from V, Y, Z. .\n\nWait, but the committee must have 2 judges and 2 scientists each year. .\n\nBut members can be new as long as they follow the constraints. .\n\nWait, but the second year\'s members don\'t have to include all the previous ones except H. They can have different members, but the chair must have been in the first year. .\n\nTherefore, the second year\'s committee could have different members, but the chair must be among F, V, Z. .\n\nFor example, if the second year\'s chair is F, that\'s okay. .\n\nAlternatively, the chair could be V. Let\'s see if that\'s possible. .\n\nIf the second year\'s chair is V (a scientist), then the committee must include V, and also follow the constraints for that year. .\n\nLet\'s try to form the second year\'s committee with ch