ints:.\n\n- Only one of I or V must be present. Here, I is present, so V can\'t be there. .\n\nSince scientists are Z and Y, that\'s okay. .\n\n- G and V: G isn\'t present, so okay. .\n\nThus, this is a valid committee. .\n\nChair is Z. .\n\nThus, Z could be a chair. .\n\nBut Z is not among the options. The options are A-F, B-G, C-Y, D-I. .\n\nSo, since the question asks which can be the chair, and the options don\'t include Z, then the only possible answer is A) F. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a case where the second year\'s chair is I (option D). .\n\nBut I wasn\'t in the first year\'s committee (since first year\'s judges were H and F), so I can\'t be the chair. .\n\nTherefore, the answer must be A) F. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nWait, but let\'s see another possibility where the first year committee includes I somehow. .\n\nWait, perhaps the first year has judges H and F, scientists Z and V, and in the second year, I is not a member, so can\'t be chair. .\n\nAlternatively, if the first year\'s second judge is F, but scientists are Z and V, then I wasn\'t there. .\n\nThus, I can\'t be chair. .\n\nTherefore, the only possible answer is A. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nWait, the problem might have a different approach. Maybe I should consider that the first year\'s committee could have different members. .\n\nWait, let me think: in the first year, the two judges are H and F, scientists V and Z. .\n\nTherefore, the first year members are H, F, V, Z. .\n\nThus, the second year\'s possible chairs are F, V, or Z. .\n\nThe options include F (A), so that\'s valid. .\n\nAlternatively, is there any way that G could be the chair? .\n\nTo be G, he must have been a member in the first year. But in the first year\'s committee, G wasn\'t a member. .\n\nThus, no. .\n\nSimilarly, Y wasn\'t in the first year\'s committee (due to H), so can\'t be chair. .\n\nI wasn\'t in the first year\'s committee, so can\'t be chair. .\n\nThus, only F is possible. .\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A. .\n\nBut wait