re, I is a judge and V is a scientist. That violates the constraint because both are present. .\n\nThus, invalid. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, second year committee: .\n\nChair F (judge). .\n\nJudges: F and G. .\n\nScientists: Z and Y. .\n\nCheck constraints:.\n\n- G and V: V isn\'t present, so okay. .\n\n- H isn\'t there, so H/Y okay. .\n\n- Only one of I or V: neither are present, so okay. .\n\nThus, this is valid. .\n\nThus, the chair could be F, which is option A. .\n\nAnother example where chair is V: .\n\nChair is V (scientist). .\n\nJudges: F and G. .\n\nScientists: V and Z. .\n\nWait, but G and V can\'t be together. Since G is a judge and V is a scientist, that\'s forbidden. .\n\nThus invalid. .\n\nAlternatively, judges F and I. .\n\nScientists: V and Z. .\n\nBut then I and V can\'t be together. .\n\nThus invalid. .\n\nTherefore, only F is possible as chair among the options. .\n\nThus, the correct answer is A) F. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nWait, but the problem\'s options include D) I. Maybe there\'s a different first year committee where I is a member. .\n\nWait, let me try another angle. .\n\nSuppose the first year\'s second judge is F, and scientists are Z and V. .\n\nThus, first year\'s members are H, F, V, Z. .\n\nNow, in the second year, can I be a member? .\n\nYes, as long as they follow the constraints. .\n\nBut to be the chair, I must have been in the first year. Since I wasn\'t there, can\'t be chair. .\n\nThus, I can\'t be chair. Therefore, my final option is: ']]  5%|5         | 30/600 [09:28<1:54:49, 12.09s/it]
