 Someone in the administration department once called Xiao Wang.\n\nHmm. Let\'s see. Xiao Wang called everyone, including himself? Or maybe not. Wait, the department includes Xiao Wang, so when he called everyone, that includes himself? That\'s a bit odd, but perhaps the problem assumes that "everyone in the department" excludes oneself. Or maybe it\'s allowed. But regardless, the question is whether someone else called Xiao Wang. Let\'s see.\n\nWe know that Xiao Li called Xiao Zhao, Xiao Zhao called no one. Xiao Wang called everyone. Are there any other people? If the department only has these three, then Xiao Wang was called by others? The only possible callers are Xiao Li and Xiao Zhao. But Xiao Zhao never called anyone, so Xiao Li could have called Xiao Wang? The problem doesn\'t state that Xiao Li called Xiao Wang. The only call we know Xiao Li made is to Xiao Zhao. Therefore, unless there are more people in the department, no one else called Xiao Wang except maybe himself. But the question is whether someone in the department called Xiao Wang. If the department includes more people, say person D, then maybe D called Xiao Wang. But since the problem doesn\'t mention other members, we can\'t assume that. However, the problem says "the administrative department of a company"—so it\'s possible that there are more people. But the given info only mentions Xiao Wang, Li, and Zhao. The problem states "everyone in the administrative department"—so when Xiao Wang called everyone, he must have called all members, including himself if applicable. But the question is whether someone else called Xiao Wang. Since Xiao Li didn\'t (unless he did, but the problem only says he called Zhao), and Xiao Zhao didn\'t, then unless there are other members, no one else did. Therefore, the answer might be no. But the option says "someone in the department once called Xiao Wang"—so if the department includes Xiao Wang himself, then he called himself, but that\'s a bit strange. Alternatively, maybe the department has at least three people, so Xiao Wang is part of it. Therefore, if Xiao Wang called everyone, including himself, then technically, he called himself, but that\'s a bit of a stretch. However, the question is about "someone in the department"—if that includes Xiao Wang, then yes, he did call himself, but that\'s probably not the intended interpretation. Alternatively, maybe the department has more people. Since the problem doesn\'t specify the exact members beyond the three mentioned, but the statements have to hold for all, we can\'t assume there are more. Therefore, under the given info, the only callers are Xiao Wang (to everyone), Xiao Li (to Zhao), and Xiao Zhao (none). So, no one else called Xiao Wang except possibly himself, but that\'s questionable. Therefore, the statement C might not hold. So C cannot be inferred.\n\nOption D: Xiao Wang answered the phone from everyone in the administration department.\n\nWait, the problem says Xiao Wang dialed everyone, but it doesn\'t say anything about him receiving calls. The third statement is about people who never answered calls from others, but it\'s about outgoing calls. So, the question is whether Xiao Wang answered all incoming calls from everyone else. But do we know that others called him? For instance, Xiao Li might have called someone else, but Xiao Li only called Zhao. Xiao Zhao didn\'t call anyone. So, the only person who called Xiao Wang would be himself (if allowed) or others not mentioned. Since there\'s no information that others called him, we can\'t assume that. Therefore, Xiao Wang might not have received any calls except from himself, but since the problem doesn\'t state that others called him, we can\'t confirm that he answered all calls. Therefore, D cannot be inferred. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
