e equator starts colder, maybe it warms up more, so at some point in the middle it could surpass the pole\'s temperature. For example, suppose the equator\'s stratosphere warms at 2°C per kilometer and is 50 km thick, while the pole\'s warms at 1°C per km and is 30 km thick. Then the middle of the equator\'s stratosphere (25 km up) would be -85 + 2*25 = -35°C, whereas the pole\'s at 15 km (middle of 30 km) would be -50 + 1*15 = -35°C. So they\'d be equal. But if the equator\'s rate is higher, maybe it could be warmer. Since the problem doesn\'t give info on rates or thickness, we can\'t assume. Therefore, C\'s statement that "at least as great" (i.e., ≥) would hold if the equator\'s middle is equal or less, but since it could be higher, C isn\'t necessarily true. Therefore, only B is definitely true.\n\nSo the answer must be B.\n\nWait, but let me check the options again. The question is which must be true. Let me confirm B:.\n\n"The temperature at any point at the top of the stratosphere is at least as great as the temperature at the top of the troposphere directly beneath that point.".\n\nYes, because the stratosphere\'s temperature increases with altitude. The top of the stratosphere is the highest point there, so it must be warmer than the starting point (tropopause). Therefore B is correct.\n\nC is comparing different locations, which depends on factors not given. A is about troposphere thickness, which isn\'t stated. D is about the top of stratosphere between poles and equator, which could depend on rates not given. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
