ion).\n- **Soy Products**: 16.9g/day (below recommendation).\n- **Dairy**: 73.6g/day (below recommendation).\n\n### Evaluation of Options:\n- **A)** \"Cereals are the most popular food.\"  \n  While cereals are the highest in consumption (335.7g), the term \"popular\" could imply preference rather than quantity. The data only measures intake, not popularity, making this **not directly inferable**.\n\n- **B)** \"Vegetables and cereals basically meet recommendations.\"  \n  Cereals are within range, but vegetables are *below* the lower limit (296g vs. 300g). Thus, this is **false**.\n\n- **C)** \"Fish/shrimp consumption is the least.\"  \n  The data shows fish/shrimp at **16.4g/day**, which is lower than soy (16.9g) and all other categories. This is **clearly true** based on the numbers provided.\n\n- **D)** \"Vegetable intake is declining yearly.\"  \n  The context states a significant drop compared to **10 years ago**, but this does not confirm a continuous yearly decline. The data could reflect a one-time drop or fluctuations, making this **unverifiable**.\n\n### Conclusion:\n**C** is the only option directly supported by the data. The other options either overstep the given information or rely on ambiguous terms. \n\n**Answer: C**",
