      "text": " C\n\n</think>\n\nBased on the context provided, we analyze each option as follows:\n\n- **Option A**: The text states that the supply-demand contradiction is \"eased to a certain extent,\" implying that the supply situation is less\u7d27\u5f20 than before. However, since consumption (300 million tons) still exceeds domestic production (175 million tons) plus imports (120 million tons), there remains a deficit. The phrase \"supply is tight\" is ambiguous but contradicts the explicit mention of easing. Thus, **A is incorrect**.\n\n- **Option B**: Domestic production (175 million tons) accounts for approximately 58% of total consumption (300 million tons), while imports (120 million tons) account for ~40%. Since domestic production constitutes the majority, the claim that consumption \"mainly depends on imports\" is false. **B is incorrect**.\n\n- **Option C**: Calculating dependence on imports:\n  - **Last year**: Imports were ~92.3 million tons (120 / 1.3), and consumption was ~267.86 million tons (300 / 1.12).  \n    Dependence: \\( \\frac{92.3}{267.86} \\approx 34.4\\% \\).\n  - **This year**: Dependence: \\( \\frac{120}{300} = 40\\% \\).  \n  The dependence has increased from 34.4% to 40%, making **C correct**.\n\n- **Option D**: The text mentions a 12% increase in crude oil consumption this year, contradicting the idea that demand is \"becoming less and less.\" **D is incorrect**.\n\n**Answer: C**",
