M1 = E + M1 → P = E. But that contradicts P > E. So no.\n\nTherefore, M2 must be higher than M1, so the first two are M2 and M1.\n\nNow, what about between Politics and English? Since P > E, Politics comes before English. So the order is M2, M1, P, E → option B.\n\nWait, but let me check the options again. The user lists the options as:.\n\nA) Major subject one, major subject two, english, politics.\n\nB) Major subject two, major subject one, politics, english.\n\nC) Major subject one, major subject two, politics, english.\n\nD) Major subject two, major subject one, english, politics.\n\nSo B is the correct one here. However, the initial problem\'s first condition says that the sum of politics and major one equals the sum of the other two (English and major two). In the example, that works, but let me see if there\'s another possibility where maybe M1 is higher than M2? But according to the deduction, that\'s impossible.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the deduction. Let me re-examine.\n\nWe had from equation (1): P + M1 = E + M2 → so M2 = P + M1 - E.\n\nThen, from condition (3): M1 > P + E → so substituting into M2\'s equation:.\n\nM2 = P + (something greater than P + E) - E → so M2 > P + (P + E) - E → simplifies to M2 > 2P. So M2 is greater than twice Politics.\n\nBut what if M1 is 100, P=40, E=30. Then M1 > 70, which is true. Then M2 would be 40 +100 -30 = 110. So M2 is 110, which is greater than M1 (100). So yes, M2 is higher.\n\nTherefore, it seems that the correct order is M2, M1, P, E → option B.\n\nWait, but looking back at the options, the user\'s question lists option C as "Major subject one, major subject two, politics, english." Which would be M1 first, but according to the deductions, that\'s not correct. So why is that an option?.\n\nHmm, perhaps I made a mistake in the deduction. Let me check again.\n\nWait, let\'s see the problem again. The first condition says the sum of political and major one equals the sum of the other two (English and major two). The second condition says the sum of political and major two is greater than the sum of the other two (English and major one). The third condition says major one is higher than the sum of political and English.\n\nWait, perhaps I need to consider that even though M2 is higher than M1, maybe the order between M1 and M2 isn\'t necessarily required? No, the deductions show that M2 must be higher than M1.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps there\'s another way to approach this.\n\nLet me try to represent the variables with inequalities.\n\nWe have:.\n\n1. P + M1 = E + M2 → Equation (1).\n\n2. P + M2 > E + M1 → Inequality (2).\n\n3. M1 > P + E → Inequality (3).\n\nLet me subtract equation (1) from inequality (2):.\n\nInequality (2) minus equation (1):.\n\n(P + M2) - (P + M1) > (E + M1) - (E + M2).\n\nSimplify left side: M2 - M1.\n\nRight side: M1 - M2.\n\nSo the inequality becomes:.\n\n(M2 - M1) > (M1 - M2).\n\nBring all terms to left:.\n\nM2 - M1 - M1 + M2 > 0 → 2M2 - 2M1 >0 → 2(M2 - M1) >0 → M2 - M1 >0 → M2 > M1.\n\nTherefore, this proves that M2 must be greater than M1. So M2 is indeed higher than M1. So that\'s settled.\n\nSo the order starts with M2, then M1.\n\nNow, what about Politics and English?.\n\nWe know from earlier that P > E.\n\nNow, we need to see where P and E are placed in the order after M1.\n\nTo see if M1 is greater than P, or if P is greater than M1, etc.\n\nWait, we know that M1 > P + E (from condition 3). Since P and E are both positive numbers, then M1 is greater than each of them individually. Because if M1 is greater than their sum, then it must be greater than each one alone. Because if, for example, P were greater than M1, then P + E would be even larger than M1, contradicting condition 3. So M1 is greater than both P and E individually.\n\nTherefore, the order so far is M2 > M1 > P > E. Which is exactly option B.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be B.\n\nWait, but let me check the options again to confirm.\n\nYes, option B is "Major subject two, major subject one, politics, english." So that\'s correct.\n\nHmm, but the original problem\'s options have option C as "Major subject one, major subject two, politics, english." So that would be M1 first, but according to the deductions, that\'s incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B. But let me check once more.\n\nAnother way to confirm is to see if any of the other options can be eliminated.\n\nOption C has M1 first, which we\'ve proven is incorrect since M2 > M1.\n\nOption A has M1 first, then M2, but M2 must come before M1.\n\nOption D has M2 first, then M1, then English before Politics, but we know Politics is higher than English. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
