site of B. But since B is the only option that provides a counterexample, perhaps the answer is none of the above, but the options must have an answer. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe I\'m missing something in the wording of the options. Let me check again.\n\nThe question says: "if which of the following is true, the above point of view can\'t be questioned?".\n\nThe correct answer is the one that, when true, the original argument cannot be challenged. .\n\nWait, option D says that fruit-eaters have larger brains because foraging is harder. This is an alternative explanation. But if that\'s true, then the original explanation (social behavior) can still be a factor. However, the existence of another factor allows people to question whether social behavior is the main cause. But the question is asking which makes the original view *can\'t* be questioned. So if there\'s another explanation, it can be questioned. Therefore, D is not the answer.\n\nHmm. .\n\nWait, perhaps the answer is C. Let me think differently. The original argument is that social behavior requires handling social relationships, so they need bigger brains. If the cerebral cortex\'s size isn\'t directly related to brain size, but the cortex is important for cognition, then maybe the brain size increase is due to other brain regions needed for social behavior. So the original argument can still hold. Therefore, if C is true, it doesn\'t affect the original argument. Wait, maybe C doesn\'t weaken the original argument. Let me parse it again.\n\nC says the cerebral cortex\'s size isn\'t directly related to brain size but is important for cognition. The original argument is about brain size being due to social behavior. If the cortex\'s size isn\'t directly related, then the social behavior\'s effect might be on other parts of the brain, so brain size could still be caused by social behavior. Therefore, C doesn\'t weaken the original argument. .\n\nWait, that\'s possible. So if C is true, it doesn\'t challenge