e non-excludable. But the answer options probably expect the best fit. .\n\nWait, the question says "public good" which in standard terms requires both. But given that the problem\'s definition only mentions non-exclusive, maybe they are simplifying. Let\'s see the options again. .\n\nExpressway (C) as a public good: if it\'s a toll road, it\'s excludable. If it\'s free, then non-excludable. Since the question doesn\'t specify, maybe in China\'s context, expressways are often toll roads. For example, in China, many expressways have tolls. So then C would be excludable. .\n\nHerdsman\'s meadows (D): since it\'s a common grazing area, it\'s non-excludable but rivalrous, so it\'s a common resource. .\n\nSocial security (A) is definitely excludable. .\n\nAffordable housing (B) is also excludable. .\n\nWait, maybe the question\'s answer is C? Or D? Hmm. Alternatively, maybe the question is using "non-exclusive" to mean both non-excludable and non-rivalrous. In that case, expressway might be considered a public good if it\'s non-toll, but again, if it\'s congested, it\'s rivalrous. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is from a textbook that defines public goods as only non-excludable. In that case, the answer would be C and D. But since the options are single choice, we need to pick the best one. .\n\nAlternatively, the term "Herdsman\'s meadows" is a common-pool resource, which is non-excludable but rivalrous, so not a public good. Expressways, if non-toll, are non-excludable but rivalrous (when congested). So neither is a pure public good. But maybe the question is considering non-excludability only. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is wrong in the options. Wait, the problem states "public good, also known as a public good" which is redundant. Maybe a typo. The standard term is "public good," so that\'s okay. .\n\nAlternatively, perhaps "Herdsman\'s meadows" is considered a club good or something else. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe "expressway" is considered a publ