erent might not directly address the two works in question. The argument\'s conclusion is that the two are not by the same poet. If there are other works by Homer that are even more different, then perhaps the two epics could still be by the same author. .\n\nBut B is a general statement that same authors can have such differences. .\n\nSince B is a direct counter to the reasoning\'s assumption, and doesn\'t depend on other works, it\'s a stronger answer. .\n\nWait, but the argument is about Homer specifically. If the hymns attributed to Homer are more different, that directly shows that Homer could have written works with such differences. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nI think I\'m going to go with B as the answer because it\'s a general principle that directly addresses the reasoning\'s assumption. .\n\nWait, but the answer is probably B. .\n\nWait, let me think of an example. Suppose someone says, "These two books are different, so different authors." .\n\nIf you can show that the same author can write books as different as those, then that weakens the argument. .\n\nTherefore, B does exactly that. .\n\nC says that other works by the same author are even more different. That also weakens, but perhaps not as directly. .\n\nIn logical reasoning, the best answer is B because it directly addresses the link between differences and different authors. .\n\nSo I think the correct answer is B. .\n\nBut I\'m really torn. .\n\nAlternatively, the answer is B. .\n\nWait, let me think of another angle. .\n\nThe original argument is based on the differences between the two poems being evidence for different authors. .\n\nIf B is true, then those differences are not evidence against the same author, because same authors can have such differences. .\n\nC provides an example of a different work by the same author that\'s even more different, so the differences between the two in question are not significant. .\n\nBoth are good, but B is more general and directly refutes the assumption. .\n\nTher