messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nThe literary works of the Chinese writer Mo Yan have made the hidden Chinese vernacular concerned by the readers of the world and recognized by the international literary world, which has once again proved that this judgment is that only the national is the world.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, based on this, which of the following options is correct?\n# Options:\nA) It's national, it's world\nB) If it is not national, it is not world\nC) As long as the nation is, it is the world\nD) Only the world is national. I think the correct option is: C\n", '<think>Okay, let\'s see. The question is about logical reasoning based on the given context about Mo Yan\'s works. The key point in the context is that his works made the Chinese vernacular recognized internationally, which proves the judgment that "only the national is the world." So the main idea here is that for something to be considered world literature, it must first be national. .\n\nThe question asks which of the options correctly represents this idea. The options are A through D. Let me go through each one.\n\nOption A says "It\'s national, it\'s world." That sounds like a direct equivalence: if something is national, then it\'s world. But the original statement says "only the national is the world," which might be a bit different. "Only the national is the world" could be interpreted as a necessary condition. Like, to be world, it must be national. So in logical terms, that would be World → National. Which is equivalent to saying if something is world, then it must be national. The contrapositive would be if not national, then not world. .\n\nOption B is "If it is not national, it is not world." That matches the contrapositive of the original statement. Since the original was "only national is world," meaning being national is necessary for being world. So B would be correct as a contrapositive. .\n\nOption C says "As long as the nation is, it is the world." This sounds like a sufficient condition: if national, then world. Which would be National → World. But the original statement was "only national is world," which is more about necessity (World → National), not sufficiency. So C might be too strong because it suggests that being national is enough, but the original might not say that all national things are world, just that to be world, you must be national. So C might not be correct.\n\nOption D: "Only the world is national." That seems to reverse the original statement. The original was "only the national is the world," so D flips it, which would be incorrect.\n\nThe original statement is "only the national is the world." In logical terms, this is equivalent to "If something is world, then it is national" (World → National). The contrapositive is "If not national, then not world," which is exactly option B. .\n\nOption A is phrased as "It\'s national, it\'s world," which is National → World, which is not the same as the original. The original is about necessity, not sufficiency. So A is incorrect because being national doesn\'t necessarily make it world, but being world requires being national. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
