Researcher2\'s would be A or ¬B: A is false, B is true → ¬B is false → so Researcher2\'s statement would be false, which is okay. .\n\nSince the question is asking which can be inferred, meaning which is necessarily true given that exactly one is true, but since both B and D are possible, but the options are single choice, perhaps the answer is D because it\'s the only one that requires both parts to be false, which is a possible scenario. Alternatively, maybe the correct answer is D. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s a different approach. Let me consider all possibilities for A and B, and see which makes exactly one of the two statements true.\n\nLet\'s define the two statements as:.\n\nS1: A ∨ B.\n\nS2: A ∨ C (assuming C is different from B, but perhaps it\'s ¬B).\n\nWait, let\'s try with S2 being A ∨ ¬B.\n\nNow, for each combination of A and B:.\n\n1. A=T, B=T → S1=T, S2=T → both true → invalid (since only one can be true).\n\n2. A=T, B=F → S1=T (A), S2=T (A) → both true → invalid.\n\n3. A=F, B=T → S1=T (B), S2=F (since ¬B=F, so A=F and ¬B=F → S2=F). → valid (only S1 true). This corresponds to option B.\n\n4. A=F, B=F → S1=F, S2=T (¬B=T) → valid (only S2 true). This corresponds to option D.\n\nThus, the valid scenarios are case3 (B) and case4 (D). Therefore, the possible answers are B or D. Since the question asks which can be inferred, meaning which must be true given that exactly one is true, but both are possible. Since the options include both B and D, but we must choose one, perhaps the answer is D because it\'s the only one that allows the second statement to be true. Alternatively, the problem might have a different intended answer. .\n\nWait, looking back at the options:.\n\nOption D says "Neither pushed into smaller orbits nor drove them out of the solar system.".\n\nAssuming that "drove them out" is the same as B (expelled), then D is ¬A ∧ ¬B, which is case4. So that\'s valid. So D is a possible answer. B is also possible. .\n\nBut the question is in C