didn\'t push into orbits, expelled them (B=T), and didn\'t drive them in (C=F). But the options don\'t mention C, so this scenario would match option B ("did not push into orbits but drove them out"), assuming that "drove them out" corresponds to B (expelled).\n\nCase2: Researcher2 is true, Researcher1 is false.\n\nResearcher2: A∨C → true.\n\nResearcher1: A∨B → false → A=F and B=F.\n\nThus, A=F, B=F, and C must be T (since Researcher2 is true).\n\nSo Jupiter didn\'t push into orbits, didn\'t expel them (B=F), but drove them into the solar system (C=T). This scenario isn\'t covered in the options, because the options only mention "drove them out of" (which would be B). Option B says "drove them out", which would be B=T, but in this case B=F and C=T (drove them in). So this isn\'t an option. Thus, this approach may not work.\n\nHmm, this is getting too convoluted. Perhaps the correct answer is D, as in the case where neither happened, making only the second statement true (if the second is A∨¬B). .\n\nGiven that in the initial analysis where S1=A∨B and S2=A∨¬B, the only valid answers are B or D, and the options include both, but since the problem is likely structured to have a unique answer, and given that when A must be false, and considering the options, D is the only one that doesn\'t involve B being true, but actually requires B to be false. However, since both are possible, but in the options, D is an option, and B is also, but the problem may require that the answer is D because it\'s the only one that allows the second conclusion to be true when the first is false. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 30%|###       | 181/600 [1:09:15<1:18:38, 11.26s/it]
