e it. So C is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D. Wait, but the original says that concealing is \"not given enough attention,\" so D says \"has not attracted the attention,\" which is a bit different. But maybe in the context, \"not given enough attention\" implies that it hasn't been sufficiently addressed, so the lack of attention is a key point. The wording in D says that it hasn't attracted the attention, which would mean that parents aren't paying attention to it at all. But the text says it's not given enough. Hmm. Maybe the answer is D because it's the closest, but I might be overcomplicating. Let me check again.\n\nAnother approach: Let's look at each option against the text.\n\nOption D: \"Children's behavior of concealing their own situation has not attracted the attention of parents.\"\n\nThe text says that the problem of concealment is \"not given enough attention.\" So, \"not given enough\" means that the attention is insufficient, but there is some attention. However, D states that it \"has not attracted the attention,\" which would mean that parents are not paying attention to it at all. That's a stronger statement than the text allows. The text says it's not given enough, but maybe some attention is given. Therefore, D might be incorrect because it's too absolute.\n\nWait, but the question is which is true of the original text. The text says it's \"not given enough attention,\" so the statement in D (\"has not attracted the attention\") would be incorrect because it's not entirely true; they have some attention, just not enough. But perhaps the options are using \"has not attracted\" as a way to say it's not given enough. Maybe in Chinese, the translation might be different, but in the English given, D might not be accurate. Hmm.\n\nLooking back at the options again:\n\nA) Parents think that children's failure to report information is more worrying than lying. \u2192 Text says lying is what they are most worried about. So A is wrong.\n\nB) Parents believe that lying is the most unforgivable moral problem. \u2192 The text says \"most worried about\" but not necessarily \"most unforgivable moral problem.\" So B is an overstatement.\n\nC) Most parents are more than willing to solve the problem of children lying. \u2192 The text says \"many have not solved it well, at least most should pay attention.\" Paying attention doesn't mean they are willing to solve it. So C is wrong.\n\nD) Children's behavior of concealing their own situation has not attracted the attention of parents. \u2192 The text says it's \"not given enough attention,\" which is not the same as not attracted at all, but maybe in the options, this is the only possible correct one. Alternatively, perhaps the question is intended to have D as correct because the text states that it's not given enough attention, which implies that it hasn't attracted sufficient attention, so D is the best answer.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is D because the text says \"not given enough attention,\" which means that parents haven't given it enough attention, so the behavior hasn't attracted enough attention, making D correct. The wording in D says \"has not attracted the attention,\" but maybe in the context of the options, it's the best fit. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is **D) Children's behavior of concealing their own situation has not attracted the attention of parents.**\n\n### Analysis:\n1. **Option A** is incorrect because the text explicitly states that lying is \"what many parents are most worried about,\" indicating it is more concerning than concealment to parents. The claim that concealment is \"more worrying\" contradicts the text.\n\n2. **Option B** is unsupported. While the text mentions lying as a major concern, it does not label it the \"most unforgivable moral problem.\" The focus is on parental worry, not moral judgment.\n\n3. **Option C** misrepresents the text. The passage states that \"many parents have not solved the problem of their children lying well,\" and while \"most [pay] attention to it,\" this does not equate to being \"more than willing to solve\" it. The text emphasizes insufficient progress, not eagerness.\n\n4. **Option D** aligns with the passage\u2019s assertion that concealment \"is not only worse solved, but also not given enough attention.\" Though \"not given enough attention\" implies some attention exists, the phrasing in the option (\"has not attracted the attention\") captures the critical point that concealment is **underaddressed**, making it the most accurate choice among the options provided. The text\u2019s emphasis on the lack of attention justifies this conclusion despite the slight nuance difference. \n\nThus, **D** is the correct answer.",
