s better because it\'s a more classic example of confirmation bias with a prediction and an event.\n\nWait, I think I\'ll go with D as the answer because the near-miss is a specific instance that the person uses to confirm their belief, whereas in A, the chef\'s belief is maintained despite information, but the question is about the process of seeking examples. The chef\'s action of using the yellow jar might not be about seeking examples, but rather holding onto a belief. Whereas in D, the event (near miss) is a confirming example that the person uses to reinforce their belief.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is A. I\'m really torn here. Let me see if there\'s any other clues.\n\nThe question says "based on the above information", so strictly applying the definition.\n\nThe key is "people try to find examples that are consistent with it and ignore those that are not.".\n\nIn A, the chef is trying to find examples (using the yellow jar and thinking it\'s better) and ignoring when it\'s not. So that\'s exactly the definition.\n\nIn D, the person has a prophecy, and the near miss is the example they use to confirm, while ignoring that it wasn\'t an actual accident. That also fits.\n\nHmm. Maybe the answer is both A and D, but since the options are single choice, perhaps the intended answer is D.\n\nAlternatively, looking at the Chinese version\'s possible nuances. The original question\'s context says that confirmation bias is when people seek consistent examples and ignore inconsistent ones. The options might have a clearer match.\n\nWait, in option D, the prophet\'s prediction was about an accident, but the near-miss isn\'t an accident. So the person is ignoring that inconsistency (the accident didn\'t happen) and instead taking the near-miss as confirmation. That\'s ignoring the inconsistent example (no accident) and focusing on the near-miss as consistent.\n\nIn option A, the chef is told that all kimchi are the same (so the inconsistent examples are the other jars), but he ignores that and focuses on the yellow jar as better. So he\'s ignoring the other jars\' effectiveness and focusing on the yellow one\'s perceived better taste.\n\nBoth fit, but perhaps the answer is A because it\'s more about the process of seeking examples. Alternatively, maybe the answer is D. Since I can\'t be 100% sure, but given common examples, D is a classic case of confirmation bias where a vague prediction is taken as confirmed by a related event. So I\'ll go with D.\n\nWait, but I\'m not sure. Let me think again.\n\nThe definition says people "try to find examples that are consistent with it and ignore those that are not.".\n\nIn A, the chef is actively seeking to confirm his belief (using the yellow jar and thinking it\'s better), thus finding consistent examples and ignoring when it\'s not. That\'s exactly the definition.\n\nIn D, the near-miss is a single event that the person interprets as confirmation, so it\'s a case of confirmation bias, but perhaps the chef\'s scenario is a better example of the process described.\n\nHmm. I think I need to choose between them. Since the question is from a test, maybe the answer is A.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see:.\n\nIn the stock manager case (B), the client\'s trust is based on the outcome, which is an example of outcome bias, not confirmation.\n\nIn the lottery (C), it\'s the gambler\'s fallacy.\n\nBetween A and D.\n\nI think the answer is D. Because confirmation bias often involves interpreting events as confirming a belief even when they don\'t fully align. The near-miss is taken as confirmation of the prophecy, which is a classic example. For instance, if someone predicts rain, and it drizzles, they take that as confirmation, ignoring that it wasn\'t a downpour as predicted. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 34%|###3      | 203/600 [1:16:49<3:00:22, 27.26s/it]
