vailable options? Alternatively, maybe the second statement is "You can visit either the Petroleum Museum or the China National Pavilion, but not both?" Not sure. Alternatively, maybe the second statement is "You must visit both the Petroleum Museum and the China National Pavilion." Wait, but that\'s just a guess. Alternatively, perhaps the second statement is "You can choose either the Petroleum Museum or the China National Pavilion, but not both." But that\'s the same as the third statement? Wait, the third statement is "Not both the China National Pavilion and the Petroleum Museum," which would be ¬(C ∧ P). So the third statement is saying that you cannot visit both C and P. So if the second statement is different, then maybe the second is saying that you can visit both? Or that they are optional in the sense that you can choose either?.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the second statement is "The Petroleum Museum and the China National Pavilion are optional [i.e., you can choose to visit either one]." Maybe that\'s phrased as "either/or" but not necessarily exclusive. Alternatively, perhaps the second statement is "You can visit both the Petroleum Museum and the China National Pavilion," so the suggestion is that you should visit both, and Wang rejects that. So the negation would be that he does not visit at least one of them. Hmm, this is a problem because the wording is unclear. Let me see the original problem again.\n\nOriginal context\'s second suggestion: "The Petroleum Museum and the China National Pavilion are optional." The Chinese term might be more precise. Alternatively, perhaps "optional" here means that you have to choose between them, i.e., you can visit one or the other but not both. Wait, that would be similar to the third statement. Alternatively, maybe "optional" here means that both are optional, so you can choose to visit either or both or neither. But that\'s a possible interpretation. Alternatively, maybe the second suggestion is "You should visit 