choose between the Petroleum Museum and the China National Pavilion, but not both." But that would conflict with the third statement. Hmm, maybe the problem has a mistranslation. Let me try to think of another way.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the second suggestion is "You can visit either the Petroleum Museum or the China National Pavilion (but not necessarily both)." So the suggestion is that you have to pick one of them, but not both. But that would be different from the third statement. Alternatively, maybe "optional" here means that both are optional, so you can visit any combination. Hmm, perhaps the second statement is saying that both are optional, so the suggestion is that you can choose to visit either or both. But the negation would be that you have to visit at least one? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe "optional" in the context means that they are optional to visit, so the suggestion is that you don\'t have to visit them. Wait, that might not make sense. Alternatively, perhaps the second statement is "You must visit either the Petroleum Museum or the China National Pavilion, but not both." Then the negation would be that he either visits both or neither. Hmm, but without knowing the exact meaning, this is tricky. Let\'s try to proceed with possible interpretations.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the second statement is "The Petroleum Museum and the China National Pavilion are optional in the sense that you can visit both." So the suggestion is that you can visit both, and Wang rejects that. So the negation would be that he cannot visit both, which is the same as the third statement. But since all three are separate, maybe the second is different.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the second statement is "You must visit at least one of the Petroleum Museum or the China National Pavilion." Then the negation would be that he visits neither. But again, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem\'s second suggestion is better translated as "The Petroleum Museum and the China National Pavilion are both must-visits" (i.e., you have to visit both), so the negation would be that he doesn\'t visit at least one of them. But I\'m just making this up.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the second statement is "You can visit both the Petroleum Museum and the China National Pavilion," so the negation is that he cannot visit both, which would mean that at least one is not visited. But that\'s the same as the third statement. Hmm, but the third statement is "Not both the China National Pavilion and the Petroleum Museum," which is exactly ¬(C ∧ P). So the third suggestion is that you cannot visit both. So if the second suggestion is different, maybe it\'s the opposite? Wait, perhaps the second suggestion is "You can visit both," so the negation would be that he cannot visit both (so ¬(C ∧ P)), but that\'s the same as the third suggestion. That can\'t be. So maybe I\'m misunderstanding the second suggestion.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the second suggestion is "You should visit at least one of the Petroleum Museum or the China National Pavilion," so the negation is that he visits neither. Hmm, but without clear wording, this is hard. Let me see the problem again.\n\nOriginal problem\'s second suggestion: "The Petroleum Museum and the China National Pavilion are optional." The term "optional" might mean that you have the option to visit them or not. So maybe the suggestion is that you can choose to visit either or both, but it\'s optional. Therefore, the negation would be that he is forced to visit both or neither? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the second suggestion is "You can visit both the Petroleum Museum and the China National Pavilion together," and the negation is that he cannot, so he must not visit at least one. But again, this is similar to the third statement.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the problem is that the second suggestion is "The Petroleum Museum and the China National Pavilion are optional, meaning you can choose to visit either one, but n