O? No, the first negation requires O. So B is missing O, which he must have visited.\n\nHmm, this is a problem. Maybe I made a mistake in interpreting the first suggestion\'s negation.\n\nFirst suggestion: If S then ¬O. The negation is S ∧ O. So yes, he must have visited both.\n\nThird suggestion: ¬(C ∧ P). Its negation is C ∧ P.\n\nThus, he must have visited S, O, C, P. But the options don\'t have an option that includes all four. The closest is B, which includes S, P, C but not O. Since the options don\'t have an option with all four, perhaps the Oil Pavilion is considered part of the Petroleum Pavilion in the options? Or perhaps the problem\'s Oil Pavilion and Petroleum Museum are the same? Let me check the original context again.\n\nOriginal context says:.\n\n"(1) if you visit the Saudi Arabia Pavilion, you won\'t visit the Oil Pavilion. (2) The Petroleum Museum and the China National Pavilion are optional. (3) Not both the China National Pavilion and the Petroleum Museum.".\n\nAh! Wait, maybe "Oil Pavilion" and "Petroleum Museum" are the same pavilion? Because "Oil" and "Petroleum" are synonyms. Maybe in the context, they are the same, so the first suggestion\'s Oil Pavilion is the same as the second and third\'s Petroleum Museum. That would make the problem solvable.\n\nIf that\'s the case, then O and P are the same variable. Let\'s redefine:.\n\nLet me redefine variables with that assumption:.\n\nLet S = Saudi Arabia Pavilion.\n\nLet P = Oil Pavilion (which is the same as Petroleum Museum).\n\nC = China National Pavilion.\n\nSo the three statements become:.\n\n1. S → ¬P (If visit Saudi, then not visit Oil/Petroleum).\n\n2. The Petroleum Museum (P) and China National (C) are optional. (Assuming optional means you can choose to visit either or both; perhaps the suggestion is you can visit both, so negation is he can\'t, meaning he must not visit at least one of them).\n\nWait but the second suggestion\'s exact meaning still matters.\n\nThird statement: Not both