ust visit exactly one of P or C," then the negation is (P ∧ C) ∨ (¬P ∧ ¬C). The third\'s negation requires P ∧ C. So that would be okay.\n\nThird\'s negation is P ∧ C.\n\nThus, combining all three negations:.\n\nFrom first: S ∧ P.\n\nFrom second\'s negation: either (P ∧ C) or (¬P ∧ ¬C). But since first requires P is true, then it must be (P ∧ C).\n\nFrom third\'s negation: P ∧ C.\n\nThus, all three together require S ∧ P ∧ C.\n\nSo he must have visited Saudi (S), Petroleum/Oil (P), and China (C).\n\nNow, looking at the options with this:.\n\nOptions with P being the Petroleum/Oil pavilion:.\n\nA) Saudi and Petroleum (P), not China (C). But according to deductions, he must have C, so invalid.\n\nB) Saudi, Petroleum (P), and China (C). That fits S ∧ P ∧ C. So this is possible.\n\nC) None of the three, so invalid.\n\nD) Didn\'t visit Saudi, Oil/P, and China. So no, invalid.\n\nTherefore, option B would be correct.\n\nBut wait, the first negation requires S ∧ P, which is satisfied in B. The third\'s negation requires P ∧ C, also satisfied. The second\'s negation (assuming the second suggestion was "exactly one of P or C") requires that he didn\'t do that, so he did both, which is okay. So B is correct.\n\nTherefore, the answer is B.\n\nBut let me confirm again with this assumption that Oil and Petroleum are the same pavilion. The problem mentioned "Oil Pavilion" and "Petroleum Museum" as separate in the context. But if they are the same, then the answer is B. Otherwise, there\'s a problem.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem\'s "Oil Pavilion" is part of the "Petroleum Museum," making them the same. Given that the options mention "petroleum pavilion," which might be the same as the Oil Pavilion, perhaps the problem intended them to be the same.\n\nGiven that without that assumption, the problem\'s options don\'t have a valid answer, I think that must be the case. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
