 the problem again.\n\nWait the question is in Chinese names and examples, maybe the translation is tricky?\n\nWait the original options:\n\nOption D: Silver and copper are both metals with electrical conductivity, but the resistivity of silver is lower, so silver is more suitable to be a conductive material than copper.\n\nThe argument here is that since they share conductivity (a), but differ in resistivity (silver has lower), so the conclusion is that silver is better. So the reasoning here is not analogical because it's not inferring a shared attribute between the two. It's using a difference to make a conclusion. So it's not the same structure as the given definition.\n\nOption B is the correct one. The user might have been confused. But let me check again the definition.\n\nThe analogical argument's form is: A has a,b,c,d. B has a,b,c. So B has d. So in B's case, the two objects (light and sound) share a,b,c (the three phenomena), and since sound has d (wave property), then light also has d. So yes, exactly.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be B. But why did the user think D was correct?\n\nAlternatively, maybe I made a mistake. Let me think again.\n\nWait the problem says \"infers that the two objects are also the same or similar in other attributes.\" So in D, they are both conductors (same attribute), and then the conclusion is that one is better. But the conclusion is not about them being the same, but about a difference. Hmm. So no.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the user thought that since they are both conductors (shared attributes) and one has lower resistivity, so it's better. But the conclusion is about suitability, which is a derived attribute. But the structure is different.\n\nHmm, maybe I was right in thinking B is correct. So the answer should be B. \n\nWait the user's initial thought was D, but according to the analysis, B is correct. Maybe the user made a mistake. But perhaps I missed something.\n\nAlternatively, maybe option C is the answer? Let me re-examine.\n\nOption C: Comparing Bethune to others who are irresponsible in work, so Bethune has a spirit. The argument is that since others have negative traits (a,b,c?), Bethune has opposite traits, so he has d (the spirit). But this seems more like a contrast, not an analogy. The conclusion is about his having a certain spirit because he's different from others. Not sure.\n\nHmm, perhaps I'm missing the structure here. Let me go back step by step.\n\nThe analogical argument requires that the two objects (A and B) share some attributes (a,b,c), and then one has an additional attribute (d) that we infer for B.\n\nSo in B: Light and sound share a,b,c (the three phenomena). Since sound has d (waves), light also has d. That's exactly the form.\n\nIn D: Silver and copper share conductivity (a). But the argument is that since silver has lower resistivity (which is a different attribute not shared), it's better. So the argument is not inferring a shared attribute, but using a difference to argue for a conclusion. So not analogical.\n\nThus, B is the correct answer. The user might have thought D was correct, but I think it's B. Therefore, the answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is **B**.\n\n### Analysis:\nThe definition of an analogical argument states that if two objects (A and B) share certain attributes (a, b, c), then we can infer that B also has another attribute (d) that A possesses. \n\n",
