      "text": " D.\n</think>\n\nThe logical relationship between Jordan and Terry\u2019s statements in the original context involves Jordan presenting a dilemma where either choice leads to a negative outcome, and Terry proposing a solution that negates one of the negative consequences through an external factor or alternative action. \n\n**Original Context Analysis:**\n- **Jordan\u2019s Dilemma:** \n  - **Option 1:** Investing in eco-practices \u2192 market share loss.  \n  - **Option 2:** Not investing \u2192 environmental harm.  \n- **Terry\u2019s Resolution:** If consumer demand forces all businesses to adopt eco-practices, the market share penalty (Option 1\u2019s downside) disappears because no single business is disadvantaged. This removes the dilemma by addressing one prong of the choice.\n\n**Option D Analysis:**\n- **Jordan\u2019s Dilemma:** \n  - **Option 1:** Rain \u2192 picnic ruined.  \n  - **Option 2:** No rain \u2192 garden dehydrated.  \n- **Terry\u2019s Resolution:** If there\u2019s no rain (Option 2), buying a hose allows watering the garden, negating the negative consequence. This removes the dilemma by introducing an action that avoids the downside of one prong.\n\n**Why Other Options Fail:**\n- **A:** Terry disputes a premise (dam bursting isn\u2019t a danger) rather than resolving the dilemma via an external factor.  \n- **B:** Focuses on comparing negatives rather than mitigating one prong\u2019s consequence.  \n- **C:** Refutes the dilemma\u2019s validity (argues both options can coexist), unlike the original where Terry accepts the dilemma but offers a resolution.\n\n**Answer:** D",
