messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nSmithtown University' s fund-raisers succeeded in getting donations from 80 percent of the potential donors they contacted. This success rate, exceptionally high for university fund-raisers, does not indicate that they were doing a good job. On the contrary, since the people most likely to donate are those who have donated in the past, good fund-raisers constantly try less-likely prospects in an effort to expand the donor base. The high success rate shows insufficient canvassing effort.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following, if true, provides more support for the argument?\n# Options:\nA) This year the average size of the donations to smithtown university from new donors when the university's fund-raisers had contacted was larger than the average size of donations from donors who had given to the university before.\nB) The majority of the donations that fund-raisers succeeded in getting for smithtown university this year were from donors who had never given to the university before.\nC) This year most of the donations that came to smithtown university from people who had previously donated to it were made without the university's fund-raisers having made any contact with the donors.\nD) Smithtown university's fund-raisers were successful in their contacts with potential donors who had never given before about as frequently as were fund-raisers for other universities in their contacts with such people.. I think the correct option is: C\n", '<think>Okay, let\'s tackle this problem. The context is about Smithtown University\'s fund-raisers having an 80% success rate, which is really high. But the argument says that this high success rate doesn\'t mean they did a good job. The reasoning is that good fund-raisers should be trying to reach out to new, less-likely donors to expand the base, not just sticking with the people who have donated before. So a high success rate might actually mean they didn\'t try hard enough to contact those less-likely prospects, because if they only contacted the easy targets (previous donors), their success rate would be high, but they aren\'t expanding.\n\nThe question asks which option, if true, would support this argument more. Let\'s look at the options one by one.\n\nOption A says that donations from new donors this year were larger than from previous donors. Hmm, not sure how this supports the argument. The argument is about the success rate being high because they didn\'t canvass enough new people. If new donations are bigger, maybe that\'s a good thing, but does it relate to the effort? Maybe not directly. The argument isn\'t about the amount, but the number of people contacted. So maybe A is not the best.\n\nOption B states that most of the donations came from people who had never given before. Wait, that seems contradictory to the original argument. The argument says that a high success rate would mean they didn\'t contact enough new people, implying that they were contacting mostly old donors. If B is true, that most donations are from new people, that would actually weaken the argument, right? Because then the high success rate would include a lot of new people, meaning they did expand the base. So B might not be the answer.\n\nOption C says that most donations from previous donors were made without the fund-raisers contacting them. Oh, this could be relevant. If previous donors are giving without being contacted, then the fund-raisers\' contacts might be targeting those previous donors. Wait, the original argument says that good fund-raisers should be trying less-likely prospects (new people) to expand. If the existing donors are donating without being contacted, then the fund-raisers didn\'t need to contact them. So if the fund-raisers were only contacting the previous donors (who would donate anyway), their high success rate is because they didn\'t reach out to new people. So if C is true, that means that the previous donors\' contributions were not due to the fund-raisers\' efforts, so the fund-raisers should have been contacting new people. Therefore, the high success rate (from the old donors they did contact) shows they didn\'t try enough new people. So C supports the argument.\n\nOption D says that the success rate with new donors for Smithtown is about the same as other universities. Hmm, the argument is saying that Smithtown\'s high overall success rate is because they didn\'t canvass enough. If their success rate with new donors is similar to others, but others might have lower overall success rates because they contact more new people (who have lower success rates), then maybe Smithtown\'s overall is higher because they focused on old donors. But D is comparing their success rate with new donors to others. If others have the same success rate with new donors, but their overall is lower, that would mean Smithtown\'s fund-raisers aren\'t worse at getting new donors, but they just didn\'t contact as many. That could support the argument. Wait, maybe D does support it?.\n\nLet me think again. The argument\'s conclusion is that the high success rate shows insufficient canvassing effort. The reasoning is that good fund-raisers should be trying to contact less-likely (new) donors, which would lower the success rate but expand the base. If Smithtown\'s fund-raisers had a high success rate, it\'s because they didn\'t try enough new people. .\n\nIf D says that when they did contact new people, their success rate was as good as other universities, that suggests that they could have contacted more new people and still had some success. But since their overall success rate is high, maybe they didn\'t contact many new people, so their high rate comes from mostly contacting old donors. So D would support the idea that their canvassing was insufficient because they didn\'t contact as many new people as they could have, given that their success with new was comparable to others. .\n\nWait, but the argument\'s point is that the high success rate indicates they didn\'t try enough. So if their success rate with new donors is as good as others, but their overall is higher, that implies they contacted fewer new donors. So D would support the conclusion. .\n\nHmm, now I\'m confused between C and D. Let me re-examine the options.\n\nThe original argument\'s key point is that a high success rate (80%) is bad because it suggests they didn\'t try enough new people. The reason given is that good fund-raisers should be trying to expand the base by contacting less likely (new) donors, which would lower the success rate. So if the fund-raisers had a high success rate, it\'s because they focused on the sure things (previous donors), not expanding. .\n\nOption C says that most donations from previous donors were made without contact. So the previous donors are donating without being asked. Therefore, if the fund-raisers were contacting previous donors, their success rate would be high, but those donations would have happened anyway. The real job is to get the people who wouldn\'t donate unless contacted. If the fund-raisers didn\'t contact many new people, their high success rate (from old donors they contacted, but who would have given anyway) shows they didn\'t do enough work. .\n\nC supports this by indicating that the previous donors don\'t need to be contacted. So the fund-raisers\' contacts are not necessary for those, so they should be contacting new people. But if they got a high success rate by contacting those who would give anyway, then they didn\'t do enough. So C strengthens the argument.\n\nOption D says their success rate with new donors is as good as others. If others have lower overall success rates because they contact more new people (which have lower success rates), then Smithtown\'s high rate is because they contacted fewer new people. So D would show that they are not worse at getting new donors, but just didn\'t try as much, which supports the idea that their high success rate is due to insufficient canvassing (not contacting new people). .\n\nWait, but does D directly support the conclusion? The conclusion is that the high success rate shows insufficient effort. If their success with new is as good as others, but others have lower overall success rates, that would mean Smithtown\'s fund-raisers didn\'t contact enough new people. So D would support that their canvassing was insufficient. .\n\nHmm. Let me see the options again. The question is asking which provides more support. Let me check each again.\n\nThe argument\'s structure: High success rate (80%) doesn\'t mean good job. Because good fund-raisers should try to contact less likely (new) donors, which would lower the success rate but expand the base. So high success rate implies they didn\'t contact enough new people. .\n\nSupport would be something that shows that the high success rate is because they focused on the easy (previous) donors and didn\'t reach out to new ones. .\n\nOption C: If previous donors are giving without being contacted, then the fund-raisers\' contacts must have been targeting previous donors (since those who were contacted gave at 80%), but the previous donors would have given anyway. So the fund-raisers didn\'t need to contact them, and thus failed to contact new people. This supports the idea that their high rate comes from not expanding, so C supports.\n\nOption D: If their success rate with new donors is same as others, but others have lower overall, that suggests that Smithtown\'s fund-raisers didn\'t contact as many new donors. Hence, their high success rate is due to less canvassing (they contacted fewer new people, so their 80% is from more old donors). So D also supports.\n\nHmm. Now I\'m stuck between C and D. Let me think again.\n\nThe conclusion is that the high success rate shows insufficient canvassing effort. The reasoning is that good fund-raisers would contact less likely (new) donors, which lowers the success rate but expands the base. So if the success rate is high, they didn\'t contact enough new people. .\n\nIf C is true, then the previous donors\' donations were not due to the fund-raisers\' efforts. So the fund-raisers\' contacts must have been with people who were already likely to donate (previous donors), but those donations would have happened anyway. Hence, the fund-raisers didn\'t need to contact them, but they did, and that\'s why their success rate is high. But they should have been contacting new people instead, which they didn\'t do. So C strengthens the argument by showing that the previous donors\' contributions weren\'t from the fund-raisers\' efforts, meaning the fund-raisers should have focused on new people but didn\'t, leading to high success rate from the old ones they contacted unnecessarily.\n\nD says that their success rate with new donors is same as others. If others have lower overall success rates because they contact more new donors (who have lower success rates), then Smithtown\'s fund-raisers must have contacted fewer new donors, hence their overall rate is higher. This supports the idea that they didn\'t canvass enough new people, hence insufficient effort. So D also supports.\n\nWait, but the question is which provides more support. The argument is specifically that the high success rate is because they didn\'t canvass enough. .\n\nOption C provides a reason why the high success rate is not due to effective work (because previous donors would have given anyway), so the fund-raisers\' contacts didn\'t add value and they didn\'t expand. .\n\nOption D provides a comparison showing that their ability to get new donors is similar to others, but since their overall is higher, they must have contacted fewer new people. So both C and D support, but which is better?.\n\nWait the original argument\'s key point is that the fund-raisers\' high success rate is due to not expanding (not contacting new people). The argument already assumes that contacting new people would lower the success rate. .\n\nOption C strengthens by showing that the previous donors\' donations were not because of the fund-raisers\' contacts. So the fund-raisers are getting success by contacting people who would give anyway, and not reaching out to new people. .\n\nOption D says that when they did contact new people, their success rate was as good as others. So their problem isn\'t that they are bad at getting new donors, but that they didn\'t contact enough of them. So D supports the idea that their low effort in canvassing (not contacting new people) is the reason for high success rate. .\n\nHmm. Let me think of it this way: the conclusion is "the high success rate shows insufficient canvassing effort." To support this, we need evidence that the canvassing effort was insufficient (i.e., they didn\'t contact enough new people). .\n\nOption D says that their success with new donors is same as others. If others contact more new people, their overall success rate would be lower. Since Smithtown\'s is higher, it must be that they contacted fewer new people. So this supports that their canvassing was insufficient. .\n\nOption C says that previous donors gave without being contacted, so the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors are unnecessary. Thus, the fund-raisers should have been contacting new people instead, but didn\'t, hence high success rate. This also supports the conclusion.\n\nSo both C and D could be contenders. Wait, but the options might have one correct answer. Let me check the options again.\n\nLooking at the options again:.\n\nC) This year most of the donations that came to Smithtown university from people who had previously donated to it were made without the university\'s fund-raisers having made any contact with the donors.\n\nThis means that previous donors are donating without being contacted. Therefore, the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors are not necessary. Therefore, if the fund-raisers were contacting previous donors, their high success rate is from people who would have given anyway. Hence, they should have been contacting new people instead. Therefore, this supports the argument that their high success rate is because they didn\'t canvass enough new people.\n\nD) Smithtown\'s fund-raisers were successful in their contacts with potential donors who had never given before about as frequently as were fund-raisers for other universities in their contacts with such people.\n\nThis means that when they did contact new people, their success rate was similar to others. But other universities might have lower overall success rates because they contact more new people (with lower success rates). So Smithtown\'s higher success rate must be because they contacted fewer new people. Therefore, their canvassing effort was insufficient, supporting the conclusion.\n\nHmm, both C and D seem to support. Wait, but maybe the answer is C?.\n\nWait, let\'s think of an example. Suppose that 100 contacts were made. 80% success rate means 80 donations. If most of the previous donors (say, 100 previous donors) donated without being contacted, then the fund-raisers\' contacts were probably with other people. Wait, maybe I need to think numerically.\n\nSuppose that the total previous donors who could be contacted are, say, 100 people. But according to C, most donations from previous donors were without contact. Let\'s say previous donors made 100 donations total, but only 20 were from people the fund-raisers contacted (and the rest 80 were from people not contacted). So the fund-raisers contacted some people (maybe previous donors and new), but their 80% success rate is from, say, 100 contacts, 80 of whom donated. But if the previous donors would have given anyway, then the 80 donations include those from people who would have given without contact. Hence, the fund-raisers didn\'t add much value, and by not contacting new people, they kept the success rate high. So C supports.\n\nAlternatively, D says that when they did contact new donors, their success rate was as good as others. Suppose other universities have a 30% success rate with new donors. If Smithtown also has 30%, but they only contacted 10 new people (3 donations), while another university contacted 100 new people (30 donations), then their overall success rates would be: Smithtown: 80% (say 80 out of 100 contacts, 70 from previous and 10 new contacts with 3 new donations). Wait, but if their total donations would be 70 (previous without contact) plus 3 new from contacts? Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe D is better because it directly compares their efforts. Since the argument is about the effort (canvassing less), D shows that their effort in contacting new people was insufficient compared to others, leading to higher success rate. .\n\nWait, but the question is which provides more support. The argument\'s main point is that high success rate implies insufficient canvassing. The support would be something that links the high rate to not contacting enough new people.\n\nOption C does this by showing that previous donors are giving without being contacted, so the fund-raisers\' contacts are redundant and they didn\'t reach out to new people. .\n\nOption D shows that their success with new is same as others, so if others have lower success rates, it\'s because they contacted more new people. Hence, Smithtown didn\'t contact enough new people. .\n\nBoth are valid, but maybe the correct answer is C? Let me check the options again. .\n\nWait, the argument says that good fund-raisers try to expand the donor base by contacting less-likely prospects. The high success rate indicates they didn\'t do that. .\n\nIf C is true, then the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors are not necessary (since those would donate anyway), so they should have been contacting new people. Since they didn\'t, their high success rate is because they focused on the easy targets, which they didn\'t need to contact. So C directly supports that the fund-raisers are not doing their job of expanding the base. .\n\nD supports by comparison, but the argument doesn\'t mention other universities. The conclusion is based on the fund-raisers\' own actions. C is more directly supporting the reasoning given in the argument. The argument\'s logic is that a high success rate means they didn\'t try new people. C gives a reason why the success rate is high (because they targeted people who would give anyway), hence supporting the conclusion. .\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is C. Wait but earlier I thought D could be. Let me see the options again.\n\nWait, the question says "provides more support for the argument". The argument\'s conclusion is that the high success rate shows insufficient canvassing effort. The support would be something that shows that the high success rate is due to not canvassing enough new people. .\n\nC says that previous donors donated without being contacted. Therefore, the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors are not needed, and thus their high success rate is because they didn\'t go after new people. This directly supports the argument. .\n\nD says that their success rate with new donors is same as others. So if others have lower overall rates because they contact more new people, then Smithtown\'s higher rate is from contacting fewer new people. This also supports. .\n\nHmm. Maybe the answer is C because it directly addresses that the previous donors\' donations were not due to the fund-raisers\' efforts, so their high success rate is from redundant contacts and not reaching new people. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe D is correct because it shows that they could have contacted more new people (since their success rate with them is as good as others) but didn\'t, hence insufficient effort. .\n\nThe argument says "good fund-raisers constantly try less-likely prospects". If their success with new is same as others, then their failure to try enough would be the reason for the high rate. .\n\nWait, maybe both are correct, but looking at the options, the answer is C. Wait, the options are from an official test? .\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the correct answer is D? Let me think again.\n\nThe argument\'s key is that the high success rate is because they didn\'t contact enough new people. To support this, we need to show that they didn\'t contact enough new people. .\n\nIf D says that their success rate with new is same as others, but their overall is higher, then they must have contacted fewer new people. Therefore, this shows insufficient canvassing. .\n\nC says that previous donors donated without contact, so the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors are unnecessary. But if the fund-raisers contacted previous donors and got donations from them, but those donations would have happened anyway, then the fund-raisers\' efforts didn\'t add value. But the problem is that the fund-raisers should be contacting new people. So the high success rate (from contacting previous donors who would give anyway) shows they didn\'t canvass enough new people. .\n\nSo both C and D support. But which one is better?.\n\nWait, the argument already states that good fund-raisers try to expand the donor base by contacting less-likely prospects. The high success rate suggests they didn\'t do that. So if C is true, then the previous donors are giving without contact, so the fund-raisers should have been contacting new people. Their high success rate is because they contacted previous donors (who would give anyway), hence not expanding. That directly supports the argument. .\n\nD is more about comparison with other universities, which the argument doesn\'t mention. The argument is about Smithtown\'s own efforts. So C is more directly relevant. .\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is C. But wait, let me check the options again. .\n\nWait, option B says that the majority of donations were from new donors. That would contradict the argument, so it\'s not B. .\n\nOption A is about donation size, which is irrelevant. .\n\nBetween C and D. .\n\nWait, the argument says that the high success rate is due to insufficient canvassing (i.e., they didn\'t contact enough new people). So if C is true, then the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors are not necessary, so they should have contacted new people. Their high success rate is because they focused on the previous donors (who would give anyway), thus not expanding. .\n\nD says that their success with new is same as others. If others have lower overall success rates, then they must have contacted more new people. Hence, Smithtown\'s high rate is because they contacted fewer new people. This also supports. .\n\nHmm. Since both seem plausible, maybe I need to see which one is better. .\n\nThe argument\'s reasoning is that good fund-raisers try to expand by contacting less-likely (new) donors, which lowers the success rate. The high success rate here implies they didn\'t do that. .\n\nIf C is true, then the fund-raisers are getting donations from previous donors who would have given anyway, so their efforts are redundant. Thus, they didn\'t try to expand. .\n\nD suggests that their ability to get new donors is okay, but they just didn\'t contact enough, hence the high rate. .\n\nBoth support, but which is better. .\n\nWait, the key point is that the high success rate is because they only contacted the sure things (previous donors). If C says that previous donors give without being contacted, then the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors are not needed. Therefore, the fund-raisers\' high success rate comes from contacting people who would give anyway, meaning they didn\'t need to contact them, but they did, and didn\'t contact new people. This directly supports the argument that the high rate shows insufficient effort (they should have contacted new people). .\n\nTherefore, C is the answer. .\n\nWait, but I\'m still a bit confused because D also seems valid. Let me see if there\'s any official explanation. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is D. Wait, let me think again. .\n\nSuppose that the fund-raisers contacted mostly previous donors. Previous donors have a high likelihood of donating. So contacting them gives a high success rate. But good fund-raisers should contact more new people (who have lower likelihood), which would lower the success rate. .\n\nIf C is true, that previous donors donate without contact, then the fund-raisers contacting them is redundant. Hence, their high success rate is because they\'re contacting people who would give anyway, not expanding. .\n\nD says that their success rate with new is same as others. Suppose other fund-raisers contact both previous and new, so their overall success rate is lower. Smithtown\'s fund-raisers have higher because they contact fewer new. .\n\nBoth C and D support, but C is more about the redundancy of contacting previous donors, while D is about comparison. .\n\nThe argument\'s reasoning is that the high success rate is due to not trying to expand. .\n\nThe correct answer is likely C because it directly addresses that the previous donors\' donations were not due to fund-raisers\' efforts, hence their high success rate is from redundant contacts and not expanding. .\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is C. .\n\nWait, but looking back, the question is which provides more support. If C is true, then the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors are not adding anything, so their high success rate is because they didn\'t try new people. So this strengthens the argument. .\n\nAlternatively, D shows that their ability to get new donors is same as others, but since their overall is higher, they must have contacted fewer new people. This also supports. .\n\nHmm, I\'m still torn. Maybe I should look for the option that directly links the high success rate to not contacting new people. .\n\nThe argument says that a high success rate is bad because it means they didn\'t contact enough new people. .\n\nC says that previous donors gave without contact. So the fund-raisers\' contacts must have been with people who would give anyway (previous), but they should have been contacting new people. .\n\nD says that when they do contact new people, their success rate is same as others. So if they contacted more new people, their overall success rate would drop, but they didn\'t contact enough. .\n\nBoth support, but the question is which is better. .\n\nIn the LSAT or similar tests, often the correct answer is the one that directly addresses the reasoning. The argument\'s reasoning is that high success rate implies not expanding the base. C directly says that previous donors don\'t need to be contacted, so contacting them doesn\'t help expand, and their high rate is because they didn\'t contact new. .\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is C. .\n\nWait, but I\'m now recalling that sometimes the correct answer is D in similar problems. Let me think of it another way. .\n\nSuppose the fund-raisers\' success rate with new donors is the same as others. If others have a lower overall success rate, it\'s because they contact more new people (who have lower success rates). Hence, Smithtown\'s higher success rate is because they contacted fewer new people. This directly shows insufficient canvassing (they didn\'t contact enough new people), supporting the argument. .\n\nSo D also does that. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe C is wrong because it talks about donations from previous donors being without contact. But the fund-raisers\' success rate is about the people they contacted. So if they contacted previous donors and got 80%, but those donors would have given anyway, then the fund-raisers\' efforts didn\'t add anything. But the argument is about not expanding the base. .\n\nSo the problem is that they didn\'t contact new people, so the base isn\'t expanded. C shows that the previous donors don\'t need to be contacted, so contacting them is a waste. But the key is that they didn\'t contact new people. .\n\nSo both C and D support. .\n\nWait, the question says "provides more support". Maybe both are correct, but looking at the options, the answer is C or D. .\n\nWait, let me check the options again. .\n\nOption C says most donations from previous donors were without contact. So the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors are not needed, so they should contact new people. Their high success rate is from contacting previous donors (who would give anyway), hence not expanding. .\n\nOption D says their success with new is same as others. If others contact more new people, their overall success rate is lower. So Smithtown\'s high rate is because they contacted fewer new people. .\n\nBoth support, but which is better? .\n\nThe argument\'s conclusion is that the high success rate shows insufficient canvassing. The support must link the high rate to insufficient canvassing. .\n\nIf D is true, then it directly shows that they didn\'t contact enough new people (since their success rate with new is same as others but their overall is higher), so that\'s a direct link. .\n\nC shows that contacting previous donors is not useful, so the fund-raisers should have been contacting new. Thus their high success rate (from previous) shows they didn\'t. .\n\nBoth are valid, but D is a more direct comparison showing that their canvassing effort (contacts with new) was insufficient. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is D. .\n\nWait, the argument says "good fund-raisers constantly try less-likely prospects". If their success with new is same as others, but they didn\'t contact as many, then they are not trying enough. .\n\nHmm. I think I\'ve overthought this. The answer is likely C. .\n\nWait, let me think of an example. .\n\nSuppose there are 100 potential donors: 50 previous and 50 new. .\n\nPrevious donors have a 90% chance of donating without contact, new have 10%. .\n\nIf fund-raisers contact 100 people: all previous donors (50), so 50 contacts, 45 donations (90%). Their success rate is 90%, very high. But they didn\'t contact any new people. .\n\nIn this case, C would be true: most donations from previous (45 out of total donations (45 + however many new donated without contact. If previous donated 45 with contact, but also 5 previous not contacted would donate, so total previous donations 50. So C says most donations from previous were without contact? Wait in this example, most previous donations (5 out of 50) are without contact? No, only 5, which is less than half. .\n\nHmm maybe my example is not good. .\n\nAlternatively, if previous donors have a high rate of donating without contact, say 80%. So of 100 previous donors, 80 would donate without contact. .\n\nFund-raisers contact 100 people, all previous donors. 100 contacts, but only 20 of previous would not have donated without contact. So the fund-raisers get 20 donations from their contacts (20% success rate?), but that contradicts. .\n\nWait, maybe my example is confusing. .\n\nAlternatively, the point is that if previous donors are donating without contact, then the fund-raisers\' contacts with them don\'t add value. Hence, the fund-raisers should contact new people. If they don\'t, their success rate is high because they\'re contacting previous, but that\'s not expanding the base. So C supports this. .\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is C. .\n\nWait, but I\'m still not sure. Alternatively, maybe the answer is D. .\n\nWait the options: .\n\nC) This year most of the donations that came to Smithtown university from people who had previously donated to it were made without the university\'s fund-raisers having made any contact with the donors.\n\nSo, most donations from previous donors were not from the fund-raisers\' contacts. That means that the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors resulted in fewer donations than the previous donors would have given anyway. Wait no, it\'s the opposite. .\n\nWait, if most donations from previous donors were without contact, then the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors only account for a minority of the previous donors\' donations. Therefore, the fund-raisers\' success in contacting previous donors is not contributing much, and their high success rate must be because they contacted people who were already going to donate. .\n\nWait, perhaps the fund-raisers only contacted a small number of previous donors, but those they contacted had a high success rate, but the majority of donations from previous donors were without contact. .\n\nThis would mean that the fund-raisers didn\'t need to contact most previous donors, so their high success rate (from the few they contacted) is not indicative of good work, and they should have been contacting new people. .\n\nHmm, maybe. .\n\nAlternatively, if the fund-raisers contacted many previous donors, but most of the donations from previous donors were without contact, that would mean that the fund-raisers\' contacts were not the reason for those donations. Hence, their high success rate (from contacting previous donors) is not helpful, and they didn\'t contact new people. .\n\nThis supports the argument. .\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is C. .\n\nWait, but I\'m still confused. Maybe I should go with C. .\n\nAlternatively, looking up similar LSAT questions, I recall that when the argument is about a high success rate indicating lack of effort in expanding, the correct answer is often the one showing that the easy targets were focused on. .\n\nIn this case, C shows that the previous donors\' donations don\'t require contact, so focusing on them (leading to high success) is not expanding. .\n\nThus, I\'ll go with C. .\n\nWait, but wait another thought: the success rate is 80% of the contacted people. If most of the donations from previous donors were without contact (C), then the fund-raisers must have contacted mostly new people? No, because if they contacted mostly previous donors, but those donations would have happened anyway, then the success rate would be high. .\n\nWait, let\'s say: .\n\nTotal previous donors: 100 people. .\n\nWithout contact, they give 80 donations (C says most of the donations from previous were without contact). .\n\nFund-raisers contacted 100 people. Suppose they contacted 50 previous and 50 new. .\n\nThe 50 previous contacted might have a high success rate, say 80%, so 40 donations. But previous donors would have given 80 anyway (from the 100). So the fund-raisers\' 40 from previous are part of the 80 total. The fund-raisers\' total donations from contacts: 40 (previous) + (success rate with new *50). .\n\nIf their overall success rate is 80%, then total donations from contacts = 80. .\n\nSo 40 + (success rate with new *50) = 80. .\n\nThus, success rate with new is (40)/50 = 80%? That would mean they had 80% success with new as well, which would be high. But C doesn\'t affect that. .\n\nHmm, this is getting too complicated. .\n\nAlternatively, the key is that if previous donors give without contact, then the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors are redundant. Therefore, the fund-raisers should focus on new donors. If they don\'t, their high success rate is because they didn\'t try new people. .\n\nThus, C strengthens the argument. .\n\nI think the answer is C. .\n\nWait, but I\'m still uncertain. Maybe the answer is D? .\n\nWait, the argument\'s conclusion is that the high success rate shows insufficient canvassing effort. To support this, we need to show that they didn\'t contact enough new people. .\n\nIf D is true, their success with new is same as others, but their overall is higher, implying they contacted fewer new people. This directly shows insufficient canvassing. .\n\nC says that previous donors give without contact, so contacting them is not needed. Hence, their high success rate (from contacting previous) shows they didn\'t contact new. .\n\nBoth are good, but D uses comparison to others to show that they contacted fewer new people, which is more directly about canvassing effort. .\n\nThe term "canvassing effort" refers to the amount of contacts made. If they made fewer contacts with new people, that\'s insufficient effort. D shows that, so it\'s better. .\n\nTherefore, maybe D is the answer. .\n\nWait, the question says "provides more support". The argument doesn\'t mention other universities, so D introduces a comparison which might be out of scope, but it\'s still valid. .\n\nAlternatively, C is better because it\'s about the donations from previous donors being without contact, so the fund-raisers\' contacts with them are redundant. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nI think I need to make a decision. Since I\'m stuck, maybe I should go with the answer that is more directly about the fund-raisers\' own efforts. .\n\nThe argument says that good fund-raisers try to expand the base by contacting less-likely prospects. The high success rate suggests they didn\'t. .\n\nIf C is true, then the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors are not adding to the donations (since those would happen anyway), so their high success rate is from redundant contacts, and they didn\'t contact new people. This directly supports the argument. .\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is C. .\n\nWait, but I\'m still not sure. Maybe the answer is D. .\n\nWait, let me think again: .\n\nThe argument\'s main point is that the high success rate indicates insufficient effort in canvassing (i.e., contacting new people). .\n\nIf D says that their success with new is same as others, then if others have lower overall success rates because they contacted more new people, Smithtown\'s higher rate is because they contacted fewer new people. This directly links the high success rate to insufficient canvassing (not contacting new people), so D supports it. .\n\nC says that previous donors give without contact, so the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous are not needed. Thus their high success rate (from contacting previous) shows they didn\'t contact new. .\n\nBoth are good. .\n\nBut the question is which provides more support. .\n\nPerhaps D is better because it directly addresses the comparison of effort. .\n\nAlternatively, the answer is C. .\n\nWait, in the original argument, the key is that good fund-raisers try less-likely prospects (new donors), which would lower the success rate. If the success rate is high, they didn\'t do that. .\n\nIf C is true, then the fund-raisers\' contacts are with previous donors (who are likely to donate anyway), so their high success rate is because they didn\'t try new people. .\n\nYes, so C is directly supporting that. .\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is C. .\n\nBut I\'m still conflicted. .\n\nWait, let me check the options again. .\n\nOption C says most donations from previous donors were without contact. So the fund-raisers\' contacts with previous donors only account for a minority of the donations from previous donors. Hence, the fund-raisers\' success in contacting previous donors isn\'t contributing much to the total, but their high success rate comes from those contacts. .\n\nThis implies that they didn\'t need to contact previous donors (since they\'d donate anyway), so their high success rate is because they didn\'t contact new people. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 40%|####      | 241/600 [1:35:14<2:09:25, 21.63s/it]
