      "text": " C.\n</think>\n\nThe argument states that Smithtown University's high success rate (80%) in securing donations does not reflect good fund-raising efforts because effective fund-raisers should aim to expand the donor base by contacting less-likely prospects (new donors), which would lower the success rate. The high success rate suggests insufficient canvassing of new prospects. \n\n**Option C** supports this by indicating that most donations from previous donors were made without fund-raisers' contact. This implies that contacting previous donors (who would donate anyway) is redundant, and the fund-raisers' high success rate stems from focusing on these easy targets rather than reaching out to new donors. This directly aligns with the argument's reasoning that the high success rate is due to a failure to expand the donor base through insufficient canvassing of new prospects.\n\n**Option D**, while also relevant, compares Smithtown's success rate with new donors to other universities. While it indirectly supports the idea of insufficient canvassing by suggesting Smithtown contacted fewer new donors, it introduces an external comparison not central to the original argument's logic. The argument focuses on the fund-raisers' own efforts, making **C** a more direct and stronger reinforcement of the conclusion.\n\n**Answer: C**",
