use the subset could differ from the larger set. \n\n**Analysis of Options:**\n- **A** is valid. The premises confirm some cathedrals are impressive and not stone, directly supporting the conclusion.\n- **B** commits a different fallacy (hasty generalization from \"some\" to \"all\").\n- **C** is valid. The existence of non-forbidding massive buildings (municipal hospitals) justifies the conclusion.\n- **D** mirrors the original flaw: It assumes that because some famous-designed buildings are poorly proportioned, government buildings (a subset of famous-designed buildings) must also include poorly proportioned ones. This is invalid, as the poorly proportioned examples might lie outside the subset.\n\n**Answer:** D",
