rong one, and see what that implies, then check which option is consistent in all cases except the one where that statement is the only wrong one.\n\nFirst, let\'s suppose that A is the wrong one, so B and C are correct.\n\nIf A is wrong, then it\'s not true that all passenger cars are overloaded. So there exists at least one passenger car that\'s not overloaded.\n\nBut B is correct, so all passenger buses are not overloaded. So that includes Ruyi\'s and Jixiang\'s buses (since they are buses), so their buses are not overloaded. But C says both Ruyi\'s and Jixiang\'s buses are overloaded. Wait, that\'s a contradiction. Wait, if B is correct, then all passenger buses are not overloaded. Therefore, Ruyi\'s and Jixiang\'s buses (as passenger buses) are not overloaded. But C\'s statement says they are overloaded, so C would be wrong. But in this scenario, we assumed that only A is wrong. Therefore, this leads to a contradiction because if B is correct, then C must be wrong, so we can\'t have both B and C correct if A is wrong. Therefore, this scenario is impossible. Therefore, A cannot be the only wrong one because it would require C to also be wrong, which violates the condition that only one is wrong.\n\nTherefore, the case where A is wrong is invalid.\n\nNext, suppose that B is the wrong one. Then A and C are correct.\n\nIf B is wrong, then it\'s not true that all passenger buses are not overloaded. So there exists at least one overloaded passenger bus.\n\nSince A is correct, all passenger cars are overloaded.\n\nC is correct, so both Ruyi\'s and Jixiang\'s buses are overloaded. So those two specific buses are overloaded, which contributes to the existence of overloaded buses, so that\'s consistent with B being wrong. So in this scenario:.\n\n- All passenger cars are overloaded (so any passenger car, including those of Ruyi and Jixiang, if they have any, would be overloaded).\n\nWait, but the options are talking about passenger cars of Ruyi and Jixiang. Wait, but C\'