n case where B is wrong, so all passenger buses are not overloaded? Wait, no. If B is wrong, then "there is no overload problem on all the city\'s passenger buses" is false. The negation of "all passenger buses are not overloaded" is "there exists at least one overloaded passenger bus." So B being wrong allows for some buses to be overloaded, but C\'s statement says both Ruyi\'s and Jixiang\'s are overloaded, which would then be true, so that\'s okay. So in that case, their buses are overloaded, but the passenger cars are all overloaded (from A).\n\nSo yes, in all valid cases, the passenger cars of both companies must be overloaded. Hence, B is correct.\n\nThe other options: Option A says they are not overloaded, which can\'t be. Option C and D suggest one is overloaded and the other not, but since A\'s statement is true in both valid cases, all passenger cars must be overloaded. So those options can\'t be. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 42%|####2     | 254/600 [1:42:29<2:29:17, 25.89s/it]
