 that the recaptured ones are a good sample. .\n\nAlternatively, C is about the tags not being removed, so that the recaptured armadillos are indeed the same ones tagged before. If tags can be removed, then maybe some recaptured armadillos are not the same ones, but that\'s about identity, not movement. Wait, the tags identify them as previously tagged. If a tag is removed, then when recaptured, it wouldn\'t be recognized as tagged. So the researcher\'s recaptured tagged armadillos must still have their tags. Therefore, to have recaptured them as tagged, the tags must not have been removed. So C is necessary because if tags can be removed, then some of the original tagged armadillos might have lost their tags and thus wouldn\'t be counted in the recaptured group. So the researcher must assume that tags stayed on, so that the recaptured tagged armadillos are indeed the same ones from before. Otherwise, maybe some tagged armadillos moved but lost their tags, so they are not in the recaptured group, making the recaptured group only those who didn\'t move. But the conclusion is about all armadillos, so the researcher must also assume that the sample is representative (D). .\n\nWait, but C is about the tags not being removed, so that the recaptured tagged armadillos are the original ones. Without C, the researcher can\'t be sure that the recaptured tagged ones are the same individuals from before. For example, if tags can be removed, maybe some armadillos that weren\'t tagged before could have tags placed by others, but that\'s unlikely. The main point is that if a tagged armadillo lost its tag, then when recaptured, it wouldn\'t be recognized as tagged, so it wouldn\'t be in the recaptured group. Therefore, the recaptured group must still have their tags, so the researcher must assume that tags weren\'t removed (C), otherwise the recaptured tagged armadillos might not include those who moved and lost tags. .\n\nSo both A and C and D could be assumptions. But which is the correct answer here?.\n\nLooking back at the options, let\'s see. The question is from the LSAT or similar, so the answer is likely A. Because the classic assumption here is that the subjects didn\'t move away and return. The movement into a new territory and back would mean that they did move, so the conclusion that they don\'t move is wrong. So A is necessary. .\n\nC is about the tags not being removed, which is also important. But the problem is that the researcher is using the recaptured tagged armadillos. If the tags can be removed, then the recaptured tagged ones are only those who kept their tags. So if some moved but kept their tags and were found far away, then the researcher would have to have captured them there. But since the researcher found most near the original spot, that suggests they didn\'t move. However, if the researcher didn\'t search far away, then those who moved might not be in the data. But the problem states that the recaptured ones were found near original spots. The key point is that the researcher must assume that the armadillos didn\'t move out and back (A). Otherwise, their presence near original spot doesn\'t mean they didn\'t move. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
