      "index": 0,
      "text": " B is correct because it explains that they eat at home less, so their total time is less, but when they do prepare, they can manage to have similar quality even with less time per dinner, perhaps by choosing quicker recipes. The option B provides an explanation that the time saved is from eating out sometimes, so the actual time spent on each dinner they do prepare isn't that less, or allows them to balance it. Alternatively, maybe the key is that the comparison is between the dinners they eat at home. So for the dinners they do eat at home, the time per dinner might be similar. Let me think of another angle: the problem says \"the dinners eaten at home by the two groups do not differ...\", so the group that works outside may prepare fewer dinners, but each of those dinners is as good as the non-workers'. The time saved is because they have fewer dinners, so the time per dinner could be the same. So if non-workers have 7 dinners and spend 210 minutes (30/meal), workers have 5 dinners and spend 150 (instead of 210-100=110?), no. Wait, the problem says workers spend 100 minutes less. So if non-workers spend 210, workers spend 110. 110 over 5 dinners is 22 per. So actually, less time per meal. So how does that not affect quality? The answer must provide a reason why less time per meal doesn't lower quality. Maybe they use more efficient methods. But the options don't say that. Option B is pointing out that they eat less often, which allows the time saved to not affect individual dinners. But I'm not sure. Alternatively, maybe option D says they spend 2 hours less on all household tasks, including dinner. But dinner is just part of that. Maybe they delegate dinner prep to others? But the option doesn't say that. Hmm. Alternatively, maybe the key is that the time saved is not all on dinner. Wait the first sentence says \"spend 100 minutes less each week in preparing dinner\"\u2014so dinner prep specifically. So D talks about all household responsibilities, which might not directly explain dinner quality. So I think B is the best answer. Let me check other views. If B is true, then the working adults eat at home less, so their total time is less because they have fewer dinners. When they do prepare, maybe they can spend enough time to make it good, but since they do it less often, the total time is less. For example, if non-workers make dinner 7 times a week taking 20 minutes each (140 total), and workers make it 5 times taking 20 each (100 total). Then they have the same time per dinner. Wait but the problem says workers spend 100 less. If non-workers spend 240, then workers spend 140. If workers have 5 dinners, that's 28 each. Hmm, not sure. Maybe the key is that the frequency of eating at home affects the comparison. The question states that the dinners they do eat at home are similar, so the fact that workers eat at home less doesn't impact the quality of those they do prepare. The time spent per dinner could be the same if their total time divided by their number of dinners equals the non-workers' time divided by theirs. So if non-workers spend X minutes on Y dinners, workers spend X-100 on Y*(0.8) dinners. For the time per dinner to be same: X/Y = (X-100)/(0.8Y). Solving, X/Y = (X-100)/(0.8Y) \u2192 X = (X-100)/0.8 \u2192 0.8X = X -100 \u2192 -0.2X= -100 \u2192 X=500. So if non-workers spend 500 minutes on 7 dinners (\u224871.4 per), workers spend 400 on 5.6 dinners (\u224871.4). Wait that would mean the time per is same. So if B is true (they eat 20% less often), then the time per dinner remains same, hence quality same. So that would resolve the discrepancy. Because the 100 minute difference is entirely due to having fewer dinners, not less time per dinner. Thus, B is the correct answer. I think I initially messed up the equations, but this shows that B allows the time per dinner to stay the same, thus explaining the quality is same. So the answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe apparent discrepancy arises because working adults spend less time preparing dinners but still achieve comparable quality. To resolve this, we need an explanation that accounts for the reduced time without compromising quality.\n\n**Option B** states that working adults eat dinner at home 20% less often. This means they prepare fewer dinners overall. If non-working adults prepare dinners, say, 7 times a week, working adults might prepare them 5\u20136 times. The **total time saved (100 minutes)** is due to fewer dinners, not less time per dinner. For example, if non-working adults spend 210 minutes on 7 dinners (30 minutes each), working adults might spend 150 minutes on 5 dinners (30 minutes each). This keeps the **time per dinner consistent**, allowing quality to remain unchanged. The reduced frequency explains the time difference without affecting individual meal preparation efficiency.\n\nOther options fail to address the core issue:  \n- **A** discusses fat content, irrelevant to the comparison of nutritional value between groups.  \n- **C** focuses on breakfast, unrelated to dinner quality.  \n- **D** mentions overall household time but does not specifically resolve the dinner-preparation paradox.  \n\nThus, **B** directly resolves the discrepancy by showing the time difference stems from fewer dinners, not rushed preparation. \n\n**Answer: B**",
      "logprobs": null,
      "finish_reason": "stop",
