generally. If the transgenic trait does not function in actual vegetables (e.g., fragrance not enhanced after injury), the method fails, invalidating the conclusion.  \n- **C)** The argument does not depend on the green fragrance being a \"self-defense weapon.\" It focuses on attracting parasitoids, not the plant's intent.  \n- **D)** Legal feasibility is irrelevant to the logical validity of reducing pesticide use. The conclusion is about efficacy, not implementation.  \n\nThus, **B** is the necessary premise to bridge the experiment (in Arabidopsis) to the conclusion (applying to vegetables).  \n\n**Answer:** B",
