      "text": " D\n</think>\n\nThe lawmaker's argument hinges on the assumption that the only benefit of the Cheese Importation Board is preventing the 1% of rejected cheese from entering Goronia, which they deem negligible compared to the agency's costs. To evaluate this reasoning, it is critical to determine whether the board provides additional benefits beyond directly rejecting shipments. \n\n**Option D** asks whether the threat of rejection deters exporters from sending substandard cheese in the first place. If the board\u2019s existence acts as a deterrent, the actual benefits of the agency could be significantly higher than the 1% rejection rate suggests. This would undermine the lawmaker\u2019s cost-benefit analysis. Conversely, if no deterrence occurs, the argument holds. Thus, this question directly addresses a key assumption in the argument. \n\nOther options are less central:  \n- **A** (disposal methods) relates to costs but does not challenge the core premise about benefits.  \n- **B** (operational efficiency) focuses on reducing costs but does not address whether the benefits are underestimated.  \n- **C** (local production) is irrelevant to the cost-benefit calculus of the board\u2019s inspections.  \n\n**Answer: D**",
