messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nAny paper involving national military, political or economic secrets is not a public paper.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, if the above assertion is true, which of the followings must also be true?\n# Options:\nA) Papers that do not involve national military, political or economic secrets cannot be made public\nB) All unpublic papers are about the national military, political or economic secrets\nC) Some papers involving national military, political or economic secrets can be made public\nD) None of the public papers are involved in national military, political or economic secrets. I think the correct option is: B\n', '<think>:.\nOkay, let\'s see. I need to solve this logical reasoning problem. The context says, "Any paper involving national military, political or economic secrets is not a public paper." Then the question is asking which of the options must be true based on that statement.\n\nFirst, I should parse the original statement carefully. The statement is a universal one: "Any paper involving.." so it\'s saying if a paper has those secrets, then it is not a public paper. In logical terms, that would be: If P (involves secrets), then Q (not public). So P → Q.\n\nNow, the options are A to D. Let\'s go through them one by one.\n\nOption A says, "Papers that do not involve national military, political or economic secrets cannot be made public." Wait, that seems to be the converse or inverse of the original? Let me think. The original is P → Q. The inverse would be if not P, then not Q. But here, the original Q is "not a public paper". Wait, the original says if a paper involves secrets (P), then it is not public (Q). So the inverse would be if a paper does not involve secrets (~P), then it is public (~Q)? Wait, actually Q is "not public", so the inverse would be ~P → ~Q? Hmm, maybe I need to rephrase. Let\'s clarify:.\n\nOriginal: If P (involves secrets), then it is not public (so Q is "not public"). So P → ¬Public.\n\nTherefore, the contrapositive would be: If it is public, then it does not involve secrets. Because contrapositive is ~Q → ~P. Since Q here is ¬Public, so ~Q would be Public. Therefore contrapositive is Public → ~P. That\'s important. The contrapositive must be true if the original is true.\n\nNow looking at the options again. Let\'s check each one.\n\nOption A: "Papers that do not involve secrets cannot be made public." That would mean ~P → ¬Public. But that\'s the inverse of the original statement. The original was P → ¬Public. The inverse would be ~P → Public? Wait, no. Wait, inverse is when you negate both. The original is P → Q (where Q is ¬Public). The inverse would be ~P → ~Q. So ~P → Public. So the inverse isn\'t necessarily true. So Option A is saying ~P → ¬Public, which is not the inverse, but actually the opposite of the contrapositive? Wait, maybe I\'m getting confused here.\n\nLet me think again. The original is P → ¬Public. The contrapositive is Public → ¬P. So, if a paper is public, it does not involve secrets. That\'s the contrapositive, which must be true. Now, looking at the options:.\n\nOption D says "None of the public papers are involved in national military, political or economic secrets." That\'s exactly the contrapositive. Because if they were involved, they couldn\'t be public. So D is correct? Let me check other options first.\n\nOption B: "All unpublic papers are about the national military, political or economic secrets." Wait, the original says that papers with secrets are not public, but it doesn\'t say that all non-public papers must have secrets. There could be other reasons a paper isn\'t public. So B is too strong; the original doesn\'t support that. So B is not necessarily true.\n\nOption C: "Some papers involving national military, political or economic secrets can be made public." But the original says any such paper is not public. So this would contradict the original statement, so C must be false.\n\nOption A: As I thought earlier, it\'s saying that if a paper doesn\'t involve secrets, then it cannot be public. Wait, that\'s the opposite of the contrapositive. Wait, the contrapositive says that if it\'s public, then it doesn\'t involve secrets. So the contrapositive allows for papers without secrets to be public or not. The original statement doesn\'t say anything about papers without secrets. So papers not involving secrets could be public or non-public. So Option A is claiming that non-secret papers cannot be public, which is not supported. The original only restricts secret papers from being public. So A is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be D. Let me confirm once more.\n\nOriginal: All secret papers are non-public. So, the set of secret papers is a subset of non-public papers. Therefore, the public papers are all non-secret. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 51%|#####1    | 307/600 [2:02:06<1:34:00, 19.25s/it]
