sures aren't taken, they'll continue working there, leading to more exposure. So that supports the idea that without measures, exposure continues and thus incidence rises. \n\nHmm, so both A and B could be contenders. Which is better?\n\nThe question asks for the best support. The expert's conclusion is based on the premise that continued exposure causes higher incidence. A supports the mechanism, making the premise stronger. B supports the premise that if no measures are taken, the exposure continues (since the environment remains polluted and people are in it). \n\nThe expert's argument is: If no measures (to improve air quality), then pollution continues, so more cases. To support this, B strengthens the link between not taking measures and continued exposure. Because B says that the improvement takes time, so without measures (i.e., no steps towards improvement), the air quality won't improve, so people stay in polluted environments. Thus, leading to more cases. \n\nAlternatively, A gives a reason why the existing exposure causes the problem, which makes the conclusion more valid. \n\nThe question is about which one \"best\" supports. Maybe both are relevant, but which is more directly supporting the conclusion. \n\nThe conclusion is about the necessity of measures to prevent increased cases. \n\nOption B directly addresses the scenario where no measures are taken leading to continued exposure. If the improvement can't happen quickly, then without any measures, the pollution isn't addressed, so exposure continues. \n\nTherefore, B supports the idea that without measures, the problem persists. A supports that the problem is indeed caused by the pollution. \n\nThe expert's argument already uses the survey data as evidence that exposure causes the diseases, so adding A would strengthen that causal link. However, the conclusion is about the future increase if no measures. To make that conclusion stronger, we need to ensure that not taking measures will lead to continued exposure. B does that by saying that without measures (since improvement takes time), people remain in polluted environments. \n\nWait, but B says \"many people have to work in a polluted environment\" regardless of measures? Or is it because the improvement takes time, so even with measures, it's slow, but without measures, it's even worse?\n\nThe wording is: \"The improvement of air quality cannot be achieved in a short period of time, and many people have to work in a polluted environment.\" \n\nThis seems like two separate statements. The first is that improving takes time, the second is that people are working in polluted areas. So even if measures are taken, they won't help soon, so people are still exposed. But the experts are saying if you don't take measures, then the problem gets worse. \n\nHmm, maybe B is not directly supporting the conclusion. Because B is about the difficulty of improving, but the expert's point is about not taking measures. If you don't take any measures, then the air quality won't improve at all, so the pollution continues. So B's first part supports that, but the second part \"many people have to work...\" is just stating that exposure is ongoing. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the key is that B's second part indicates that people are already in polluted environments, so if you don't take measures, they stay there, leading to more cases. That does support the expert's point. \n\nComparing to A: if A is true, then the mechanism exists, so the current correlation (from the survey) is indeed causal, so the future projection (more cases without measures) is valid. \n\nBoth A and B support different parts. Which is better?\n\nThe question asks for the option that best supports the experts' opinion. The expert's conclusion is a prediction based on the premise that continued exposure leads to more cases. To support this prediction, we can either strengthen the causal link (A) or ensure that continued exposure will occur if measures aren't taken (B). \n\nSince the argument already has the survey showing that exposure causes the problem, the missing link might be that without measures, exposure will continue. Therefore, B provides that link by stating that without measures (since improvement takes time and people are in polluted areas), the exposure continues. \n\nAlternatively, if the argument's premise is that \"continuous exposure causes these issues,\" then the need for measures is to stop the exposure. If B says that without measures, the exposure continues (because you can't improve air quickly and people are in it), that directly supports the conclusion. \n\nTherefore, the best answer is B. \n\nWait, but initially I thought A was better. Hmm. Maybe I need to recheck the options again.\n\nOption A: Explains why exposure causes the problem (mechanism). This strengthens the causal relationship between pollution and disease, making the expert's prediction more credible. \n\nOption B: Explains why not taking measures would lead to continued exposure (since improvement takes time and people are in polluted areas), thus leading to more cases. \n\nBoth are supporting, but in different ways. The question is about which is the best support. \n\nThe expert's argument is: If no measures \u2192 exposure continues \u2192 more cases. \n\nTo support this, either:\n\n1. Show that the exposure actually causes the disease (A), or\n\n2. Show that without measures, exposure continues (B). \n\nThe first (A) supports the causal link between exposure and disease, while B supports the link between no measures and continued exposure. \n\nThe argument already uses the survey data to establish that exposure causes some incidence. The survey shows that continuous exposure leads to 10-15% getting the diseases. So the survey already provides evidence of causation. Therefore, the gap in the argument might be the assumption that without measures, the exposure will continue. \n\nTherefore, B is better because it addresses the 'if no measures are taken' part, ensuring that the exposure will indeed continue. \n\nHence, the correct answer should be B. \n\nWait, but the survey already says \"continuous exposure to high concentrations...\" So the experts are saying if we don't improve, the exposure continues, so cases increase. So the key is that not taking measures leads to continued exposure. B supports that by saying that improving takes time (so without measures, it won't improve), and people are in polluted environments. \n\nTherefore, I think B is the best choice. \n\nOption A is also relevant, but since the question is about the expert's opinion that the incidence will increase if no measures are taken, the critical point is that the measures are needed to reduce exposure. B directly links the lack of measures to continued exposure. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B. \n\nWait, but the first part of B says \"improvement cannot be achieved in a short period\", so even if measures are taken, it might not help quickly, but the expert's conclusion is about not taking measures at all. If you don't take any measures, then the air quality won't improve, so exposure continues. So B's first part supports that without measures, the air won't get better. The second part says many people are working in polluted environments, which also supports that exposure is ongoing. \n\nSo putting it together, B is a better support. \n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is B. My initial thought was correct, but I confused myself by considering A. \n\nAlternatively, another angle: The expert says that if urgent measures are not taken, the incidence will rise. The support needed is that the absence of measures leads to more cases. \n\nOption B states that the improvement takes time, so without measures, people remain in pollution \u2192 more cases. \n\nOption A explains why the pollution causes the problem, which is good, but the survey already provided that. The question's expert conclusion is about the future increase, so ensuring that exposure continues is key. \n\nYes, so B is better. \n\nFinal Answer: B\n</think>\n\nThe expert's argument is that without urgent measures to improve air quality, the incidence of chronic eye inflammation and xerophthalmia will increase due to continuous exposure to pollutants. To support this, the argument must establish that the absence of measures will lead to ongoing exposure.\n\n**Option A** explains the mechanism by which toxic particles damage eyes, reinforcing the causal link between pollution and the diseases. However, the survey already provides this correlation, so this strengthens an existing premise rather than directly addressing the conclusion's dependency on future exposure.\n\n**Option B** states that improving air quality takes time and many people work in polluted environments. This directly supports the expert's claim by highlighting that without urgent measures, exposure will persist (since improvements are delayed and people remain in polluted areas), leading to increased incidence. This bridges the gap between \"no measures\" and continued exposure, which is critical to the conclusion.\n\n**Option C** introduces pollen season as a factor, which might weaken the argument by suggesting an alternative cause, making it irrelevant. \n\n**Option D** provides demographic data but does not address the link between pollution and disease incidence.\n\nThus, **B** most effectively supports the expert's opinion by confirming that the absence of measures will result in prolonged exposure, thereby increasing disease rates.\n\n**Answer: B**",
