ternatively, the answer is A because the earthquake is the catalyst that the paragraph is highlighting as the reason the requirements were revived. .\n\nIn the absence of a combined option, I think the answer is C because the paragraph\'s last sentence is the critical part that defines the moral requirements, which is the main takeaway. The earthquake is the trigger but not the main point. .\n\nWait, but the first sentence says the principles were being masked, so the earthquake brought them back. The main idea is that the earthquake caused the revival. The explanation of what they include is to clarify what was revived. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nThis is quite challenging. Let me think of it this way: which option does NOT include incorrect information? .\n\nOption D is about reporting the earthquake in accordance with ethics, which is not the main point. .\n\nOption B is too generic. .\n\nBetween A and C. .\n\nThe paragraph says the earthquake "revived the moral requirements.. the morality here is not only.. but also.." So the main point is that the earthquake revived the requirements, which include more than just ethics. .\n\nThe best summary is the one that captures both aspects. Since that\'s not an option, the next best is C because it explains the components of the morality, which is the key detail the paragraph emphasizes. The earthquake is the reason for their revival, but the paragraph\'s focus is on what the requirements are. .\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is C. .\n\nAlternatively, the question might consider A because it\'s the main event. .\n\nWait, the first sentence says the assembly line approach has masked the necessity of many principles (the moral requirements). The earthquake has revived them. So the main point is the revival caused by the earthquake, hence A. .\n\nThe explanation of what the morality includes is to define the requirements that were revived. .\n\nThus, the best summary is A. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nI think I\'m going to have to decide. .\