the team must include Y, so that can't be. But if M is host, it's okay. So B is possible. So maybe B is not the answer.\n\nOption C: Z and X. Team is Z and X. Host is either Z or X.\n\nIf Z is the host, then according to Z's statement (2), he must invite Y or M. But in this team, the member is X, which isn't Y or M. Therefore, that's a problem. So Z can't be host here.\n\nIf X is the host, then according to X's statement (3), Y must be a member. But in the team Z and X, the members are X (host) and Z, so Y isn't there. Therefore, X can't be host here either. \n\nAlternatively, is there another possibility? Wait, the host must be one of them. So since Z is host, but that would require inviting Y or M, which they didn't. If X is host, but requires Y, which isn't present. So this combination can't happen. Therefore, option C is impossible. Wait, but let me check again.\n\nWait, the team is Z and X. If Z is host, he must invite Y or M, but the member is X, so that's invalid. If X is host, he must have Y as a member, but Y isn't there. Therefore, neither can be the host, so this combination is impossible. So option C would be the answer?\n\nWait, but let me check the options again. The options are the members, not considering the host. Wait, actually, the team includes the host, so the combination would be two people with one being the host. So for example, if the team is Z and X, the host has to be either Z or X. But in both cases, the conditions aren't met, so this combination can't exist. So option C is impossible. \n\nOption D: Y and M. The team is Y and M. So host is either Y or M.\n\nIf Y is the host, then according to Y's statement, he must invite Z or X. The member here is M, so that's not Z or X. Therefore, that would violate Y's condition. \n\nIf M is the host, then according to M's statement, he must have X or Y as a member. Here, the member is Y, so that's okay. So if M is the host, and Y is a member, then the team is M and Y, which is allowed. \n\nTherefore,