easible, so boat is necessary. So B might not be necessary because the argument already states that overland was extremely difficult. Wait, the problem is that the argument says "the vast mountainous jungle.. would have made overland travel extremely difficult centuries ago". So the argument is saying that overland was very hard, so the only possible way is boat. Therefore, the assumption is that there\'s no other way besides boat. But perhaps the author is assuming that boat travel was possible between those regions. Because maybe the two regions aren\'t even connected by sea? Wait, Mexico and Peru are on the same coast, so they are along the Pacific. So maybe boat travel is possible. But the argument doesn\'t mention that, so maybe that\'s an assumption. Wait, but the options don\'t have that. Let me check the options again.\n\nLooking at B: "it was easier to travel by boat between western mexico and coastal peru than to travel by an overland route." The argument says overland was extremely difficult, so maybe the author is assuming that boat travel was possible and thus the better option. But the conclusion is that they "must have been transported by boat", so the assumption might be that boat was possible. But the question is about an assumption required. Alternatively, the argument\'s reasoning is that because overland was too hard, the only way is boat. Therefore, the assumption is that there are no other possible routes. But maybe there are other ways besides overland or boat, like air? But the dogs can\'t fly. So the only options are overland or boat. Since overland is too hard, boat is the only way. Therefore, the argument\'s assumption is that overland wasn\'t feasible, which is stated, so perhaps the assumption is that boat travel was possible. Wait, but the problem is that the options don\'t have that. Let me look again.\n\nOption B says it was easier by boat than overland. The argument\'s premises already state that overland was extremely difficult. So if overland was extremely difficult, then boat would be the only feasible way. Therefore, the author might be assuming that boat travel was possible. But that\'s not one of the options. Alternatively, maybe the author is assuming that the only possible way is boat, so the assumption is that overland wasn\'t possible. But the argument says "extremely difficult", not impossible. So maybe the author assumes that even though overland is extremely difficult, it\'s not possible enough, so boat is required. So the assumption is that the overland route\'s difficulty makes boat the only viable option, which is essentially what B is saying. Because if boat wasn\'t easier than overland, then maybe people would have tried overland despite the difficulty, but since it\'s extremely difficult, the only option is boat. Wait, but "easier" might not be the same as "only possible". The argument\'s conclusion is that they "must have been transported by boat", so the assumption is that there\'s no other way. The overland is too hard, so boat is the only way. So the assumption is that boat travel was possible between the regions. But the options don\'t mention that. Hmm.\n\nLooking at option B again: "easier to travel by boat.. than overland". Since the argument says overland was extremely difficult, the author might be assuming that boat was a viable alternative, which would be easier. So B is stating that boat was easier, which the argument\'s reasoning requires. Because if boat wasn\'t easier, even if overland was hard, maybe they couldn\'t do boat either, so maybe the dogs couldn\'t have been transported. Wait, but maybe the argument is assuming that boat travel was possible (i.e., feasible), which would make it the only option. Since the author\'s conclusion is that they were transported by boat, they must assume that boat travel between those regions was possible. But the problem is that the options don\'t have that. Let me check the options again.\n\nOption B is about it being easier than overland. The argument\'s reasoning is that because overland was extremely difficult, boat must have been used. So the assumption is that boat was possible. But the argument might not need to assume that it was "easier", just that it was possible. Alternatively, if boat travel wasn\'t possible, then the conclusion falls apart. So the assumption is that boat travel between the regions was possible. Since that\'s not an option, maybe B is the closest. Because if boat travel wasn\'t easier than overland (even if overland is hard), maybe people would have tried overland, but since it\'s extremely difficult, they couldn\'t. So the author might not need to assume that boat was easier, just that it was possible. Hmm, this is tricky.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the argument assumes that there\'s no other possible way besides boat or overland. For example, maybe the dogs could have been transported by air, but that\'s impossible. So the only options are boat or overland. Since overland is too hard, boat is the only way. So the assumption is that there are no other possible means. But that\'s not an option here. .\n\nLooking at option C: "If hairless dogs were transported by boat, they were traded in exchange for other goods." The argument mentions trading expeditions, so maybe they assume that trade occurred. But the conclusion is about transportation by boat, so maybe the assumption is that trade happened, but C is a conditional statement. The argument\'s conclusion is that they were transported by boat during trading expeditions. So maybe the assumption is that such trading expeditions occurred, but C is saying that if they were transported by boat, then they were part of a trade. That might not be necessary. The argument could say they were transported as part of exploration, not trade. So C might not be required.\n\nOption D: "Most of the trade goods.. were transported by boat." The argument doesn\'t need to assume that most trade was by boat, just that boat was pos