messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nA, B, C, D and E are on duty. Each person is on duty for one day from Monday to Friday. Requirements: B is in front of D and E, and C cannot be on Tuesday. There is exactly one day between A and D.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, ask B is on duty what time of week is the five people on duty unique?\n# Options:\nA) Monday\nB) Tuseday\nC) Wednesday\nD) Thursday. I think the correct option is: A\n', '<think>Okay, let\'s try to tackle this problem step by step. So, we have five people: A, B, C, D, and E. Each is on duty for one day from Monday to Friday. The requirements are:\n\n1. B must be before D and E. So, B has to come earlier than both D and E in the schedule.\n2. C cannot be on Tuesday.\n3. There\'s exactly one day between A and D. That means if A is on, say, Monday, D has to be on Wednesday (since there\'s Tuesday in between). Or if A is on Tuesday, D would be Thursday, etc.\n\nThe question is asking, if B is on duty on which day would the schedule be uniquely determined? The options are Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, or Thursday.\n\nHmm, so first, I need to figure out for each possible day B could be on (the options given), whether that leads to only one possible arrangement. The answer is the day where only one valid arrangement exists when B is placed there, considering all the constraints.\n\nLet me start by considering each option one by one.\n\nStarting with Option A: B is on Monday.\n\nIf B is on Monday, then D and E have to be after Monday. Let\'s see the other constraints. The key one is the one between A and D. There must be exactly one day between them. So, A and D can\'t be adjacent; there must be exactly one day in between. So possible pairs for A and D would be:\n\nIf A is Mon, D would be Wed (but B is already on Mon, so A can\'t be Mon here). Wait, since B is on Monday, A can\'t be Monday. So A could be Tuesday, then D would be Thursday. Or A on Wednesday, D on Friday. Or A on Thursday, but then D would be Saturday, which isn\'t in the schedule. So possible A and D pairs when B is Monday are:\n\nA on Tue → D on Thu,\n\nA on Wed → D on Fri,\n\nA on Thu → D on Sat (invalid), so those are the only two possibilities.\n\nAdditionally, C can\'t be on Tuesday. Let\'s consider the first case where A is Tue and D is Thu.\n\nSo days:\n\nMon: B\n\nTue: A\n\nWed: ?\n\nThu: D\n\nFri: E or C?\n\nWait, the people not yet placed are C and E. Since B must be before D and E, so E has to be after B. So E can be on any day after Monday, but also, we have to place C. But C can\'t be on Tuesday. Let\'s see:\n\nIf A is Tue, then:\n\nMon: B\n\nTue: A\n\nThen Wed can be C or E. But C can\'t be on Tuesday, which is already okay here. Let\'s see:\n\nIf Wed is C, then Thu is D, and Fri must be E. That works because B is before D and E. So that\'s one arrangement:\n\nMon: B, Tue: A, Wed: C, Thu: D, Fri: E.\n\nAlternatively, if Wed is E, then:\n\nMon: B, Tue: A, Wed: E, Thu: D, Fri: C. Wait, but C can\'t be on Tuesday, which is okay here. But then D is on Thu, which is after E on Wed. But the requirement is that B is before D and E, which is satisfied here. However, there\'s another constraint: the one between A and D. In this case, A is Tue and D is Thu, which is exactly one day apart (Wed in between), so that\'s okay. But here, E is on Wed, which is after B (Mon), so that\'s okay. So this would be another arrangement:\n\nMon: B, Tue: A, Wed: E, Thu: D, Fri: C.\n\nWait, but then C is on Friday, which is allowed. So in this scenario, there are two possible arrangements when B is on Monday and A is on Tuesday. Because the placement of E and C on Wednesday and Friday can vary. Therefore, that\'s two possibilities here. \n\nAlternatively, if A is on Wednesday, then D would have to be on Friday (since there\'s one day between them). So let\'s see:\n\nMon: B\n\nTue: ?\n\nWed: