f A is on Wednesday, D on Friday. Alternatively, if D is on Monday and A on Wednesday? Wait, but since B is on Tuesday, D has to be after B (since B must be before D). So D can\'t be on Monday or Tuesday. So D has to be on Wed, Thu, Fri. \n\nWait, so D must be after Tuesday. So the possible pairs where A and D have exactly one day between them:\n\nIf A is on Mon, then D must be on Wed (since Tue is B\'s day). \n\nIf A is on Wed, then D must be on Fri. \n\nAlternatively, if A is on Thu, then D would need to be on Sat (invalid). \n\nAlternatively, could D be before A? Let\'s see. For example, if D is on Wed, then A could be on Fri? But that would have two days between them (Thu and Fri? Wait, the difference between Wed and Fri is two days (Thu in between), so that\'s exactly one day between. Wait, yes. Wait, the distance between Wed and Fri is two days apart (Wed to Fri is two days, with Thu in between). So if D is on Wed and A on Fri, that\'s also one day between. So that\'s another possibility.\n\nWait, so actually, the A and D can be in either order as long as their positions are two days apart. But since D must be after B (which is on Tuesday), D can be Wed, Thu, Fri. \n\nSo possible scenarios:\n\nCase 1: A is on Monday, D on Wednesday.\n\nThen, the schedule so far:\n\nMon: A\n\nTue: B\n\nWed: D\n\nThu: ?\n\nFri: ?\n\nRemaining people are C and E. They have to be placed on Thu and Fri. Also, C can\'t be on Tuesday (which is already okay here). Also, B is before D and E. Since D is on Wed, which is after B, and E must also be after B. So E can be on Thu or Fri. \n\nSo possible arrangements here:\n\nThu: C and Fri: E → \n\nMon:A, Tue:B, Wed:D, Thu:C, Fri:E\n\nOr Thu:E and Fri:C → \n\nBut C can\'t be on Tuesday, so that\'s okay. However, does C have any other restrictions? The only restriction is C not on Tuesday, so both are okay. Thus, two possibilities here.\n\nAlternatively, maybe there\'s another constraint? Let\'s see:\n\nWait, in this case, if A is Monday and D is Wednesday, then E has to be after B (which it is). So those two options are possible. So this gives two arrangements already.\n\nCase 2: A is on Wednesday, D on Friday.\n\nThen:\n\nMon: ?\n\nTue: B\n\nWed: A\n\nThu: ?\n\nFri: D\n\nRemaining people are C and E. They need to be on Mon and Thu (since Mon is open, and Thu is open). Wait, Mon is available? Wait, A is on Wednesday, so Mon is open. Wait, in this case, the days:\n\nMon: ?\n\nTue: B\n\nWed: A\n\nThu: ?\n\nFri: D\n\nSo remaining people are C and E. They can be placed on Mon and Thu, or Mon and Fri? Wait, Fri is D. So Mon and Thu. So:\n\nMon: C or E,\n\nThu: the other.\n\nAlso, C can\'t be on Tuesday, which is fine here. \n\nSo possibilities:\n\nMon: C, Thu: E → \n\nMon:C, Tue:B, Wed:A, Thu:E, Fri:D\n\nOr Mon:E, Thu:C → \n\nMon:E, Tue:B, Wed:A, Thu:C, Fri:D\n\nSo again two possibilities here.\n\nCase 3: D is on Thursday. Wait, how would that work?\n\nWait, if D is on Thursday, then A must be two days before or after. \n\nIf D is on Thursday, then A could be on Tuesday (but B is on Tue), so no. Or A on Tuesday is taken. So A would have to be on Tuesday, which is B\'s day. Not possible. Alternatively, A could be on Saturday? No. Wait, maybe D is on Thursday, then A must be on Tuesday (but taken) or on Saturday (invalid). Alternatively, if A is on Friday, then D would need to be two days before, so Wednesday. Wait, maybe I need to think differently. \n\nAlternatively, if D is on Thursday, then A must be two days away. So A could be Tuesday (but B is there) or Saturday (invalid). So that\'s not possible. So D can\'t be on Thursday in this scenario? Wait, maybe I missed something. Let\'s see:\n\nWait, if D is on Thursday, then A could be on Tuesday (but that\'s B\'s day). So that\'s not possible. So perhaps D can\'t be on Thursday in this case. So maybe only the two cases above (D on Wed or Fri).\n\nAlternatively, another possibility where A is on Thursday, but then D would have to be on Saturday, which is invalid. So that\'s not possible.\n\nAnother case where A is on Friday, then D would have to be on Wednesday (since Fri minus two days is Wed). So D would be on Wed. Then:\n\nMon: ?\n\nTue:B\n\nWed:D\n\nThu: ?\n\nFri:A\n\nThen remaining are C and E for Mon and Thu.\n\nSo:\n\nMon: C or E,\n\nThu: the other.\n\nThus, two possibilities again.\n\nWait, so in this scenario, when B is on Tuesday, there are multiple possibilities. For example:\n\nIn case of A on Mon and D on Wed, two options.\n\nIn case of A on Fri and D on Wed, two options.\n\nIn case of A on Wed and D on Fri, two options.\n\nSo total of multiple arrangements. So Option B (Tuesday) isn\'t the answer.\n\nHmm, moving on to Option C: B is on Wednesday.\n\nWait, if B is on Wednesday, then D and E must be after Wednesday, so on Thursday or Friday.\n\nThe requirement is exactly one day between A and D. Let\'s see.\n\nFirst, since B is on Wednesday, D must be Thu or Fri. \n\nLet\'s consider possible A and D positions.\n\nThe one-day between A and D can be in various ways. Let\'s see:\n\nPossible scenarios:\n\nCase 1: D is on Thursday. Then A must be two days before or after.\n\nIf D is Thu, then A could be Tue (since two days before: Tue to Thu has Wed in between, which is exactly one day between). Or A could be Sat (invalid). So A must be on Tuesday.\n\nBut then, let\'s see:\n\nIf A is on Tue, D on Thu. Then:\n\nMon: ?\n\nTue: A\n\nWed: B\n\nThu: D\n\nFri: ?\n\nRemaining people are C and E. They need to be placed on Mon and Fri. Also, C can\'t be on Tue, which is okay.\n\nSo:\n\nMon: C or E,\n\nFri: the other.\n\nBut also, B must be before E. Since B is on Wed, E must be on Thu or Fri. But D is on Thu, so E can be Fri. So:\n\nPossible arrangements:\n\nMon: C → then Fri: E\n\nOr Mon: E → Fri: C. \n\nBut E has to be after B (Wed), so both are okay. So two possibilities here.\n\nAlternatively, could A be on Friday? Then D would have to be on Wednesday (since Fri minus two days is Wed). But D has to be after B (Wed), 