      "text": " D.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is **D**. \n\n**Explanation:**  \nThe context provides two key statements:  \n1. **If a language is fully effective (LE), then every combination of basic languages can form independent words (ACWB).**  \n   *(LE \u2192 ACWB)*  \n2. **If the auditory system has problems (AP), then not every combination can form independent words (\u00acACWB).**  \n   *(AP \u2192 \u00acACWB)*  \n\nFrom these, we can derive:  \n- If the auditory system has problems (AP), then \u00acACWB must hold.  \n- Since LE requires ACWB (from the first statement), if \u00acACWB is true, then LE must be false. Therefore, **AP \u2192 \u00acLE**.  \n\nThis directly matches **Option D**: \"If the human auditory system has problems receiving sound signals, then language cannot be fully effective.\"  \n\n**Analysis of Other Options:**  \n- **A:** Incorrect. A functioning auditory system (\u00acAP) does not guarantee ACWB or LE, as other factors could prevent effectiveness.  \n- **B:** Unsupported. The context does not link \"effectiveness of language\" to \"practicality of communication\" explicitly.  \n- **C:** Incorrect. It assumes the converse of the first statement (ACWB \u2192 LE), which is not logically valid unless stated as a biconditional.  \n\nThus, **D** is the only logically consistent choice.  \n\n<\uff5cAnswer\uff5c>  \nD  \n<\uff5c/Answer\uff5c>",
