      "text": " C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is **C**. \n\n**Explanation:**  \nThe principle in option C states that when scandals are seen as the responsibility of parties rather than individual incumbents, voters penalize the responsible party when possible. This aligns with the context:\n\n1. **When all parties are blamed equally:** Voters cannot penalize all parties simultaneously (since they must vote for someone), so incumbents from all parties are re-elected. The principle accounts for this by noting that responsibility lies with the parties, but penalizing all is impractical, leading to no penalty (re-election).\n\n2. **When only one party is blamed:** Voters can effectively penalize that single party by defeating its incumbents. The principle explains this as targeting the responsible party when possible.\n\nOther options fail to address the contrast:\n- **A** incorrectly focuses on challengers' identities, which the context does not mention.\n- **B** discusses the accuracy of judgments, which is irrelevant to the described voter reactions.\n- **D** contradicts the first scenario, as incumbents blamed collectively are still re-elected despite the principle suggesting they should not be.\n\nThus, **C** best explains the contrast in voter behavior. \n\n**Answer:** C",
