messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nFor example, X X Group held an anniversary celebration, your mobile phone number won a grand prize of 100000 yuan. this kind of unwise fraudulent text messages on mobile phones still appear again and again even after they have been exposed by the media. Professional swindlers prefer to use shoddy fraudulent text messages rather than design more deceptive and gullible text messages, which only shows that the swindlers are too stupid and unprofessional.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following options, if true, strongly refutes the above conclusion?\n# Options:\nA) A swindler must be smart, otherwise it is impossible to deceive others.\nB) When swindlers cheat, they will try their best not to attract the attention of the police.\nC) If a deception was useless, the swindler would have eliminated it long ago.\nD) Swindlers use such text messages to catch people who may be fooled, and they want to filter out smart people in the first place.. I think the correct option is: A\n', '<think>Okay, let\'s see. The problem here is about logical reasoning. The context says that even though those fake messages from the X X Group about winning a prize are exposed by media, they keep appearing. The conclusion is that the swindlers are too stupid and unprofessional because they don\'t design better, more deceptive messages.\n\nThe question is asking which option, if true, would strongly refute that conclusion. So we need to find an option that shows that the swindlers are actually smart or professional, despite using these simple messages.\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nOption A: "A swindler must be smart, otherwise it is impossible to deceive others." Hmm, this seems to state a general rule, but does it refute the conclusion? The conclusion says they are stupid. If A is true, then maybe they have to be smart, so if they are swindlers, they must be smart. But does that directly counter the argument? The original argument says that using these messages shows they are stupid. If A is true, then maybe the fact that they are swindlers (so they do deceive) implies they are smart, so the conclusion is wrong. But this might be a bit indirect. Not sure yet.\n\nOption B: "When swindlers cheat, they will try their best not to attract the attention of the police." This is about avoiding police attention. The original argument is about the messages being unwise because they are obvious. If they use simple messages to avoid police notice, maybe that\'s a smart strategy. But the conclusion is that they are unprofessional because they don\'t make better messages. If B is true, perhaps the reason they don\'t make better messages is to stay under the radar, which is a smart move, so this could refute the conclusion. But I need to think more.\n\nOption C: "If a deception was useless, the swindler would have eliminated it long ago." The original argument says these messages are still around, so they must be ineffective, hence the swindlers are stupid. But if C is true, then if the messages were useless, they would have stopped using them. Since they are still using them, that means they are effective. So the author\'s premise (that the messages are unwise and thus show stupidity) is wrong because actually the messages are working, so the swindlers are smart to keep using them. This seems like a strong refutation. Because the conclusion is based on the idea that the messages are ineffective, but C says that if they were ineffective, they wouldn\'t be used. Therefore, their continued use implies effectiveness, so the swindlers are actually smart, not stupid. That directly attacks the conclusion\'s reasoning.\n\nOption D: "Swindlers use such text messages to catch people who may be fooled, and they want to filter out smart people in the first place." So the idea here is that the messages are designed to target only those who are gullible, which is a smart strategy. The original argument assumes that using simple messages is a sign of incompetence, but D says it\'s intentional to filter out the smart people. That way, the messages are actually strategic, so the swindlers are professional. This also refutes the conclusion.\n\nSo now between C and D. Let me re-examine the original conclusion. The conclusion is that the swindlers are too stupid and unprofessional because they don\'t design better messages. The author thinks that since the messages are exposed and still used, they must be bad. But if C is true, then the messages must be working (otherwise they would have been dropped), so the continued use shows they are effective, so the swindlers are actually smart. That\'s a good point.\n\nD is saying that the messages are a way to filter out the smart people, so they\'re intentionally using a method that only the gullible would fall for. That also means it\'s a strategic choice, so the swindlers are professional. .\n\nHmm, which one is a stronger refutation? The conclusion\'s main point is that the swindlers are not smart because they don\'t make better messages. If C is correct, the messages are actually effective, so the swindlers are keeping them because they work, so they are smart. D says they are using it to target a specific group, which also shows strategy. .\n\nThe original argument\'s premise is that the messages are "unwise" and "shoddy", so the author assumes that these messages are not effective. But if C is true, the fact that they are still used means they are effective, so the premise that they are unwise is wrong. Therefore, the conclusion that the swindlers are stupid is wrong. .\n\nD provides an alternative reason why they use the messages (to filter), which also undermines the conclusion. But does D require that the messages are effective? Maybe the messages are designed to only appeal to the less smart, so even if they are not very effective overall, they are targeting a subgroup. .\n\nThe question is which one "strongly refutes". .\n\nC directly addresses the effectiveness: if the messages were useless, they would have been discarded. Since they are still around, they are useful, so the author\'s assumption that the messages are bad is wrong, so the conclusion that swindlers are unprofessional is wrong. .\n\nD says it\'s a filtering method, which is a different angle but still shows intentionality. .\n\nI think both C and D could work, but maybe C is a more direct refutation. Let me check the options again. .\n\nWait, the conclusion is that the swindlers are "too stupid and unprofessional" because they don\'t design better messages. The author\'s reasoning is that since the messages are exposed and still used, they must be bad, hence the swindlers are not smart. .\n\nIf C is true, then the messages must be effective, so the swindlers are not stupid. That directly contradicts the conclusion. .\n\nD says they are using them to filter, which might mean that even if the messages are not very effective overall, they are effective in a certain way. But the author\'s point is that the messages are not deceptive enough. If D is true, then the swindlers are being strategic, so they are professional. .\n\nHmm, both options can refute, but perhaps D is better because it provides an intentional reason why they use such messages, showing that it\'s a deliberate strategy, hence professional. .\n\nWait, the original argument says "Professional swindlers prefer to use shoddy fraudulent text messages rather than design more deceptive and gullible text messages, which only shows that the swindlers are too stupid and unprofessional." .\n\nThe author thinks that using shoddy (poor quality) messages instead of better ones shows lack of skill. But if D is true, then using shoddy messages is actually a way to filter, so it\'s a professional strategy. Therefore, D directly addresses the premise that they should design better messages, by saying that the current messages are designed to filter, so they are actually better in their approach. .\n\nC says that if the messages were useless, they would have been eliminated. Since they are still around, they are useful, so the swindlers are smart. That also works. .\n\nI think both C and D are possible. Wait, looking at the options again:.\n\nOption C: If a deception was useless, the swindler would have eliminated it long ago.\n\nThe original argument says that these messages are still used even after being exposed. The author thinks that implies they are bad. But if C is true, then since they are still used, they must be useful, so the author\'s conclusion is wrong. .\n\nTherefore, C is a good refutation. .\n\nD says that they use it to filter out smart people. That\'s another reason, so even if the messages are not very effective overall, they are effective in targeting a subset. So the swindlers are using a smart strategy. .\n\nSo both C and D can refute, but which one is stronger? .\n\nThe question asks which "strongly refutes". .\n\nThe conclusion is that the swindlers are stupid because they don\'t make better messages. .\n\nIf C is true, the continued use implies effectiveness, so they are not stupid. .\n\nIf D is true, the messages are intentionally designed to target a certain group, so it\'s a professional strategy. .\n\nEither could work, but maybe D is better because it explains why the messages are kept despite being exposed: because they are part of a filtering process. .\n\nAlternatively, C is more straightforward. .\n\nLooking at the options again, perhaps D is the answer because it directly addresses the reasoning that using simple messages is unprofessional. The author assumes that better messages would be more effective, but D says that the simple ones are used to filter, so it\'s a deliberate choice. .\n\nTherefore, D would show that the swindlers are actually professional in their approach, hence refuting the conclusion. .\n\nHmm. I\'m a bit torn. Let me think again. The conclusion is that the swindlers are unprofessional because they don\'t design better messages. If the messages are actually effective (as per C), then that\'s a refutation. If they are using them for filtering (as per D), that\'s another refutation. .\n\nWhich one is a stronger refutation? .\n\nThe problem is that the conclusion is based on the premise that the messages are ineffective. If C is true, then the messages are effective, so the premise is wrong. .\n\nIf D is true, it\'s possible that the messages are not very effective overall but target a specific group. But even so, if they are still effective enough to make money, then the swindlers are smart. .\n\nAlternatively, D\'s point is that the messages are designed to filter out the smart people, so the swindlers are targeting those who are more likely to fall for it. That\'s a strategic move, which would be professional. .\n\nSo maybe both are valid, but in the options, perhaps D is better. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe C is better because it directly addresses the effectiveness. .\n\nWait, the original argument says that the messages are "shoddy" and "unwise", implying they are not working well, but continue to be used. The author thinks that\'s because the swindlers are not smart enough to make better ones. .\n\nIf C is true, then the fact that they are still used shows that they are working (since if they didn\'t work, they would have been dropped). So the swindlers are keeping them because they are effective. Thus, they are smart enough to keep using what works, so the conclusion is wrong. .\n\nD says that they use them to filter, so even if the messages are not very effective, they are effective in filtering. So maybe the overall success rate is lower, but it\'s a targeted approach. .\n\nBut the key is that the author thinks the messages are bad because they are exposed and still used. C counters that by saying that if they were bad, they would have been dropped. Since they are still used, they must be good. .\n\nTherefore, C is a more direct refutation. .\n\nWait, but the original problem states that the messages "still appear again and again even after they have been exposed by the media." The author thinks that exposure should lead to their discontinuation if they were ineffective. But according to C, if they were ineffective, they would have been eliminated. Since they are still around, they must be effective, so the swindlers are smart. .\n\nTherefore, C is the best choice. .\n\nWait, but D also provides an alternative reason. Let me check the options again. .\n\nWait, the options are:.\n\nA) A swindler must be smart, otherwise it is impossible to deceive others.\n\nB) When swindlers cheat, they will try their best not to attract the attention of the police.\n\nC) If a deception was useless, the swindler would have eliminated it long ago.\n\nD) Swindlers use such text messages to catch people who may be fooled, and they want to filter out smart people in the first place.\n\nSo D is saying that the messages are used to filter out the smart people, so the target is those who are easily fooled. This way, the messages are designed to be simple so that only the gullible respond. So the swindlers are actually smart because they don\'t want to waste time on people who are too smart. Thus, their strategy is to use simple messages to filter, which is a professional approach. This would mean that the original conclusion that they are unprofessional is wrong because they are using an intentional strategy. .\n\nTherefore, D also refutes the conclusion. .\n\nSo which is better? .\n\nThe question is which "strongly refutes". .\n\nThe conclusion is that the swindlers are "too stupid and unprofessional" because they don\'t design better messages. .\n\nIf C is true, then the messages are effective (since they are still used), so they are not stupid. .\n\nIf D is true, then the messages are part of a deliberate strategy (filtering), so they are professional. .\n\nBoth are valid, but maybe D is a better refutation because it provides an intentional reason for using the messages, showing that it\'s a professional tactic. .\n\nAlternatively, C attacks the premise that the messages are ineffective. .\n\nHmm. Since the conclusion is based on the idea that the messages are bad (because they are exposed and still used), but if C is true, then the messages are actually good (since they are still used), so the swindlers are smart. .\n\nTherefore, C is the correct answer. Wait but D also is a possible answer. .\n\nWait, the original problem\'s conclusion is that the swindlers are unprofessional because they don\'t design better messages. .\n\nIf the answer is D, it\'s saying that the messages are designed to filter out smart people, so it\'s a deliberate strategy. Therefore, the swindlers are being professional by using this method. Hence, D refutes the conclusion. .\n\nAlternatively, if C is correct, it\'s saying the messages are effective, so they are kept, so the swindlers are smart. .\n\nBoth options seem correct, but maybe the intended answer is D. .\n\nWait, let me think of it this way: the author\'s reasoning is that since the messages are "shoddy" (simple, easy to spot), but they keep using them even after exposure, so they must be stupid for not improving. .\n\nIf D is true, then the reason they don\'t improve the messages is because they want to target only those who are easily fooled, so the simplicity is intentional to filter out the smart ones. Therefore, this is a professional strategy, so the conclusion is wrong. .\n\nC says that if the messages were useless, they would have been eliminated. Since they are still used, they are useful. So the author is wrong in thinking that the messages are ineffective. .\n\nSo both attack different parts of the argument. .\n\nThe original argument\'s conclusion is based on the premise that the messages are not good (shoddy) and thus the swindlers are unprofessional. .\n\nIf C is true, the premise that the messages are not working is wrong because they are still used, so they must be working. .\n\nIf D is true, the premise that not designing better messages is unprofessional is wrong because it\'s a deliberate choice for filtering. .\n\nBoth are valid, but perhaps D is a better refutation because it directly addresses the reasoning about why they don\'t make better messages. The author assumes that better messages would be more effective, but D says that the current messages are better for their purpose (filtering). .\n\nTherefore, D is the answer. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is C. .\n\nHmm, this is a bit tricky. Let me check similar logical reasoning questions. .\n\nThe structure is: .\n\nPremise: Swindlers use simple messages despite exposure. .\n\nConclusion: Therefore, they are unprofessional/stupid. .\n\nWe need to find an option that weakens this. .\n\nOption C provides that if the messages were useless, they would have been dropped. Since they are still used, they are useful. Therefore, the swindlers are keeping effective messages, so they are not stupid. .\n\nThis directly attacks the link between the continued use and the effectiveness. So the author\'s assumption that the messages are ineffective is wrong. .\n\nOption D provides an alternative reason for using the messages (to filter), which also weakens the conclusion by showing it\'s a strategy. .\n\nIn logical terms, C undermines the premise that the messages are ineffective, while D undermines the reasoning by providing an alternative explanation. .\n\nWhich is stronger? .\n\nThe conclusion is based on the idea that using simple messages indicates lack of skill. .\n\nIf C shows that the messages are actually effective (so the swindlers are skilled enough to keep using them), that directly refutes the conclusion. .\n\nD shows that there\'s another reason (filtering), so even if the messages aren\'t the most effective, the strategy is smart. .\n\nBut the original conclusion is that they are unprofessional because they don\'t design better messages. If D is true, then designing better messages would not be the goal, so their choice is professional. .\n\nTherefore, D also works. .\n\nHmm. Maybe the answer is D. .\n\nAlternatively, let\'s see the options again. .\n\nThe question is to refute the conclusion that the swindlers are "too stupid and unprofessional". .\n\nIf D is correct, then the swindlers are using the messages for a strategic reason, so they are professional. .\n\nIf C is correct, the messages are effective, so they are smart. .\n\nEither way. .\n\nBut the options are presented as single answer. .\n\nLooking at the options, perhaps the answer is D. .\n\nWait, the original argument says "Professional swindlers prefer to use shoddy fraudulent text messages rather than design more deceptive and gullible text messages, which only shows that the swindlers are too stupid and unprofessional." .\n\nThe conclusion is that using shoddy messages instead of better ones shows they are unprofessional. .\n\nIf D is true, then the reason they don\'t use more deceptive messages is because they want to filter out smart people. Thus, their choice is intentional and professional. .\n\nTherefore, D directly addresses the reasoning that choosing shoddy over better messages is unprofessional, by providing a professional reason for the choice. .\n\nC attacks the premise that the messages are ineffective, but if D is true, even if the messages are effective, the reason for using them is strategic. .\n\nBut the key is that the conclusion is about the swindlers\' intelligence and professionalism. .\n\nEither way, both options can refute. .\n\nWait, the problem might be from a standard test. Let me think of similar questions. .\n\nIn such cases, the answer is often the one that provides an alternative explanation for the observed behavior that is more professional. .\n\nSo D would be the answer here. .\n\nAlternatively, the answer is C. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, let\'s think of it this way: .\n\nThe author\'s reasoning is that since the messages are exposed and still used, the swindlers must be stupid. .\n\nC says that if the messages were useless, they would have been eliminated. Since they are still used, they must be useful. So the swindlers are keeping effective messages, so they are smart. .\n\nThus, C directly contradicts the author\'s assumption that the messages are ineffective. .\n\nTherefore, C is a strong refutation. .\n\nD is also good, but perhaps C is more directly related to the effectiveness. .\n\nIn the options given, I think the answer is C or D. .\n\nWait, the question is from a Chinese test, perhaps the answer is C. .\n\nAlternatively, let\'s think of it this way: the author says that because the messages are still used even after exposure, they are unprofessional. .\n\nIf C is true, the messages must be working (since otherwise they\'d be dropped), so the continued use shows effectiveness, hence the swindlers are smart. .\n\nTherefore, C is the answer. .\n\nAlternatively, D is also correct. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nWait, another angle: the conclusion is that the swindlers are "unprofessional" because they don\'t design better messages. .\n\nIf D is true, then their choice of messages is part of a professional strategy (to filter), so they are professional. .\n\nThus, D directly addresses the professionalism. .\n\nTherefore, D is better. .\n\nI think I need to decide between C and D. .\n\nThe question is which one "strongly refutes the conclusion". .\n\nThe conclusion\'s key point is that the swindlers are unprofessional because they use shoddy messages instead of better ones. .\n\nIf D is correct, then the use of shoddy messages is a professional strategy, so they are professional. .\n\nTherefore, D is the best answer. .\n\nYes, I think D is the correct answer here. .\n\nWait, but let me check the wording of D again: .\n\n"Swindlers use such text messages to catch people who may be fooled, and they want to filter out smart people in the first place.".\n\nThis means that the messages are designed to only attract those who are easily fooled, thus filtering out the smart ones. .\n\nTherefore, it\'s a targeted approach, which is a professional tactic. Hence, the swindlers are professional, so the conclusion is wrong. .\n\nTherefore, D is correct. .\n\nC also works, but I think D is better. .\n\nWait, the original argument says "Professional swindlers prefer to use shoddy.. rather than design more deceptive and gullible text messages.." .\n\nWait, maybe there\'s a translation issue. The original Chinese might have nuances. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is C. .\n\nHmm. .\n\nAlternatively, the answer is C because it directly addresses the premise that the messages are ineffective. .\n\nIf the messages were useless, they would have been eliminated, so their continued use implies they are useful. Therefore, the author\'s assumption that the messages are ineffective is wrong, so the conclusion that the swindlers are stupid is also wrong. .\n\nTherefore, C is the correct answer. .\n\nI think I need to make a decision. .\n\nIn some similar LSAT questions, the answer would be C because it directly attacks the link between continued use and ineffectiveness. .\n\nAlternatively, D provides a reason why they are kept. .\n\nI think the answer is C. .\n\nWait, but let me think again. .\n\nThe conclusion is that the swindlers are unprofessional because they don\'t make better messages. .\n\nIf C is true, the messages are effective, so they are smart to keep them. .\n\nIf D is true, the messages are part of a strategy, so they are professional. .\n\nEither way, but which is stronger. .\n\nThe question is to "strongly refute" the conclusion that they are unprofessional. .\n\nD directly provides that their choice is a professional strategy. .\n\nC provides that they are effective, so they are smart. .\n\nBoth are good, but D is more about the professionalism. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 73%|#######3  | 439/600 [2:56:28<3:13:33, 72.13s/it]
