itions**:\n   - Total boys (B_total) > Total girls (G_total).\n   - Total students in science classes (S_total) > Total students in liberal arts classes (LA_total).\n\n2. **Analyzing Options**:\n   - **Option A**: The total number of boys in the liberal arts class is more than the total number of girls in the liberal arts class.\n     - Example: All boys in science and all girls in liberal arts. This makes boys in LA 0 and girls in LA positive, so A is false.\n   - **Option B**: The total number of boys in the science class is more than the total number of girls in the science class.\n     - Example: Equal boys and girls in science with sufficient boys in liberal arts to satisfy B_total > G_total. This makes B false.\n   - **Option C**: The total number of boys in the liberal arts class is higher than that of girls in the science class.\n     - Example: Minimal boys in liberal arts and many girls in science. This makes C false.\n   - **Option D**: The total number of boys in the science class is more than that of girls in the liberal arts class.\n     - Testing various scenarios consistently shows that boys in science must exceed girls in liberal arts to satisfy B_total > G_total and S_total > LA_total. \n\n3. **Verification**:\n   - Multiple scenarios were tested where D was always true. For instance, if girls in liberal arts are maximized, boys in science must be sufficiently high to maintain B_total > G_total, ensuring D holds.\n\nThus, the correct answer is:\n\n\\boxed{D}",
