messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nIf you close all the wrong doors, truth will be shut out.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is closest to the meaning of the above judgement?\n# Options:\nA) If truth is shut out, it must be that you close all the wrong doors.\nB) Truth can not be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed.\nC) If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed.\nD) To accept the truth, we must reject wrong.. I think the correct option is: A\n', '<think>Okay, let me try to work through this problem step by step. The original statement is: "If you close all the wrong doors, truth will be shut out." So the structure here is an "if.. then.." statement. In logical terms, this can be represented as P → Q, where P is "closing all the wrong doors" and Q is "truth will be shut out.".\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options is closest in meaning to this original statement. The options are A, B, C, or D. Let me go through each one.\n\nStarting with option A: "If truth is shut out, it must be that you close all the wrong doors." This seems like it\'s reversing the original implication. The original was P → Q, so A is Q → P. In logic terms, that\'s the converse of the original statement, which isn\'t necessarily equivalent. So A might not be the right answer because the converse isn\'t the same as the original implication.\n\nOption B: "Truth cannot be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed." Hmm, "unless" can be tricky. Let me parse this. The structure here is "Truth cannot be shut out unless all wrong doors are closed." In logical terms, "unless" translates to "if not," so "Truth can\'t be shut out" (¬Q) unless "all wrong doors are closed" (P). Wait, actually, "unless P, then ¬Q" would be equivalent to Q → P? Let me think again. The standard form is "A unless B" is equivalent to "if not B, then A." So "Truth cannot be shut out unless all wrong doors are closed" would be "Unless all wrong doors are closed, truth cannot be shut out." Which is "If not P, then truth cannot be shut out (¬Q)." Wait, maybe I\'m getting confused here. Let me rephrase B: "Truth cannot be shut out (¬Q) unless (meaning if not) all wrong doors are closed (P)." So "¬Q unless P" translates to "If not P, then ¬Q." Wait, but the original was P → Q. Let me see:.\n\nOriginal: P → Q (If P, then Q).\nOption B: "Truth can\'t be shut out (¬Q) unless P" → "¬Q only if P" → "If ¬Q then P?" Or maybe "¬Q → P"? Hmm, I\'m a bit confused here. Let me try to structure it properly.\n\nThe phrase "unless all the doors of wrong are closed" (P) is the condition. So "Truth cannot be shut out (¬Q)" unless P. So the statement is: "Truth cannot be shut out (¬Q) unless P" → which would mean that if P is not done, then truth cannot be shut out. In other words, if ¬P, then ¬Q. Which is equivalent to Q → P. Wait, that\'s the same as option A. So perhaps I\'m making a mistake here. Let me check again.\n\nAlternatively, maybe "Truth cannot be shut out unless all the wrong doors are closed" is equivalent to saying "To have truth not shut out (¬Q), you must close all wrong doors (P)." So ¬Q → P. Which is again the converse of the original implication. But the original was P → Q, so if P then Q. The converse is Q → P, but here it\'s ¬Q → P? Hmm, maybe I\'m mixing up.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct translation is "Truth can be shut out only if all wrong doors are closed." Wait, the original statement is "If you close all the wrong doors, then truth will be shut out." So closing all wrong doors (P) leads to truth being shut out (Q). So P → Q. .\n\nOption B says "Truth can\'t be shut out unless all wrong doors are closed." Let me parse "unless" as "if not," so "unless all wrong doors are closed" translates to "if not P, then truth can\'t be shut out." So "If not P, then ¬Q." Which is equivalent to P ∨ ¬Q, which is the same as P → Q. Wait, that\'s the original statement! Because "If P, then Q" is equivalent to "If not P, then it doesn\'t necessarily mean anything about Q?" Wait no, actually, in logic, P → Q is equivalent to ¬P ∨ Q. So "If not P, then Q can still be true or false?" Wait, no. The equivalence is that P → Q is the same as "Either P is false, or Q is true." So the statement "Truth cannot be shut out unless all wrong doors are closed" would be "Unless you close all wrong doors (P), truth cannot be shut out (¬Q)." So "Unless P, ¬Q" → which would be "If ¬P, then ¬Q". But "If ¬P then ¬Q" is equivalent to Q → P, which is the converse of the original. Wait, but the original is P → Q. So maybe I\'m getting this wrong.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps I should re-express option B. Let me try to see:.\n\nOriginal statement: If P (close all wrong doors), then Q (truth is shut out). So P → Q.\n\nOption B: "Truth cannot be shut out (¬Q) unless all wrong doors are closed (P)." So the structure is: ¬Q can happen only if P is true. Wait, that\'s a bit confusing. Let me think of it as "You can only have ¬Q (truth not shut out) if P is not done." Hmm, maybe not. Alternatively, "Truth cannot be shut out" (¬Q) unless P (closing all wrong doors) is done. So the only way for ¬Q to be true is if P is done? Wait, that doesn\'t make sense. Let me try another approach.\n\nSuppose the original is P → Q. The contrapositive of that is ¬Q → ¬P. Which would mean "If truth is not shut out (¬Q), then you did not close all wrong doors (¬P)." That\'s the contrapositive, which is logically equivalent. So the contrapositive is a valid equivalent statement.\n\nLooking at the options, option C is: "If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed." Wait, that\'s exactly the contrapositive. "If ¬Q then ¬P." So that\'s equivalent. So option C is the contrapositive, which is logically equivalent. But the question is asking which is closest to the meaning of the original. So that would be C?.\n\nWait, but let me check the options again. Let me read option C again: "If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed." The original was "If you close all the wrong doors, truth will be shut out." So the contrapositive is "If truth is not shut out, then you did not close all the wrong doors." So that\'s exactly option C. So C is equivalent.\n\nBut let me check the other options again to be sure.\n\nOption D: "To accept the truth, we must reject wrong." Hmm, that\'s a bit of a stretch. The original is about closing doors leading to truth being shut out, so maybe this is a different angle. The original statement\'s contrapositive is C, which is equivalent, so that\'s probably the answer. But let me see the options again.\n\nWait, but let me check option B again. The original is P → Q. Option B says "Truth cannot be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed." So "Truth can\'t be shut out (¬Q) unless P." The "unless" here might be confusing. Let me parse it as "Truth cannot be shut out (¬Q) unless P (closing all wrong doors)" → which would mean that ¬Q can only occur if P is done? Or is it that to have ¬Q, you must have P?.\n\nAlternatively, "Truth cannot be shut out unless all wrong doors are closed" might mean that the only way for truth to be shut out (Q) is by closing all wrong doors (P). Which would be Q → P. Which is the converse of the original, so that\'s not equivalent. But if the original is P → Q, then Q can happen for other reasons too. So B is not equivalent. So perhaps C is the right answer.\n\nWait, but let me check the wording of the options again carefully.\n\nOriginal statement: If you close all the wrong doors, truth will be shut out. So P → Q.\n\nOption C: If the truth is not shut out (¬Q), then the door to all wrong must not be closed (¬P). So ¬Q → ¬P. Which is exactly the contrapositive, so that\'s equivalent. Therefore, C is correct.\n\nOption B: "Truth cannot be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed." The structure here is "Truth cannot be shut out (¬Q) unless P (closing doors)." So "unless P" means "if not P, then ¬Q cannot happen?" Wait, maybe it\'s better to parse it as "Truth can be shut out (Q) only if P (closing doors)." Because "unless" can sometimes be tricky. Let me think again.\n\nThe standard translation for "A unless B" is "If not B, then A." So "Truth cannot be shut out unless P" would be "If not P, then truth cannot be shut out." So "¬P → ¬Q". Which is equivalent to P ∨ ¬Q, which is the same as P → Q. Wait, that\'s the original statement! Because P → Q is equivalent to ¬P ∨ Q, but here we have ¬P → ¬Q, which is equivalent to P ∨ ¬Q. Wait, that\'s different. Because the original is P → Q (which is equivalent to ¬P ∨ Q), while B\'s translation would be ¬P → ¬Q (equivalent to P ∨ ¬Q). These are different unless Q is equivalent to P, which it isn\'t. So B is actually equivalent to the inverse of the contrapositive? Hmm, perhaps I made a mistake here.\n\nWait, let me clarify:.\n\nOriginal: P → Q (If P then Q).\n\nOption B\'s translation: "Truth cannot be shut out (¬Q) unless P" → "¬Q unless P" → "If not P, then ¬Q" → ¬P → ¬Q → which is equivalent to P ∨ ¬Q (same as P → ¬¬Q → P→Q? Wait, no. Wait, ¬P → ¬Q is equivalent to P ∨ ¬Q. The original is P → Q, which is equivalent to ¬P ∨ Q. So the two statements are different. So B is actually different from the original. Therefore, B is not equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer must be C, the contrapositive. Because the contrapositive is logically equivalent to the original implication.\n\nSo the answer should be C. But let me check the options again.\n\nOption C: "If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed." Yes, that\'s exactly ¬Q → ¬P. So that\'s the contrapositive, so correct.\n\nBut wait, the original problem\'s context says "If you close all the wrong doors, truth will be shut out." So the contrapositive is "If truth is not shut out, then you did not close all the wrong doors," which is exactly option C. Therefore, the answer is C.\n\nHowever, let me check the other options again to be thorough.\n\nOption D: "To accept the truth, we must reject wrong." This seems to introduce a new concept of "accepting truth" and "rejecting wrong," which isn\'t directly mentioned in the original statement. The original is about closing doors leading to truth being shut out. So D might be a distractor, not directly related.\n\nOption A is the converse, which isn\'t equivalent, so not A.\n\nOption B\'s translation led to a different statement, so not B. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 80%|#######9  | 478/600 [3:13:12<44:26, 21.86s/it]
