nA) Definition into defined items (twice?), probably a mistake. Maybe \"defined term, defining term, and definiendum?\" Not sure. But likely incorrect.\n\nB) Hohhot into districts: decomposition, since districts are parts.\n\nC) Celestial bodies into natural and man-made: division, correct.\n\nD) Penalty into principal, deprivation, etc. Maybe the principal is a category, and the rest are types of penalties. But in some legal systems, principal punishments are the main ones (like death penalty, imprisonment), and the others are additional. So the division here isn't properly into species. The correct division would be principal vs. accessory, but the option lists three categories where two are accessory. So the division here isn't correct. \n\nTherefore, the answer is C. The user might have thought B is correct because it's splitting into parts, but according to the given definitions, division requires species concepts, so B is decomposition. So the correct answer is C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is **C**. \n\n**Explanation:**  \nAccording to the definitions provided, **division** requires splitting a concept into **species concepts** (subcategories) that are mutually exclusive and collectively exhaustive under a common genus. \n\n- **Option B** describes splitting Hohhot into districts, which are **parts** of the city, not subcategories. This is an example of **decomposition** (breaking a whole into parts), not division.  \n- **Option C** categorizes celestial bodies into **natural** and **man-made**, which are distinct species under the broader concept of \"celestial bodies.\" This aligns with the definition of division.  \n\nOther options:  \n- **A** contains redundancy and does not clearly define a proper division of \"definition.\"  \n- **D** mixes principal punishments with accessory penalties (e.g., deprivation of rights), which are not all species of the same level.  \n\nThus, **C** is the only valid example of logical division. \n\n**Answer:** C",
