      "text": " B.\n</think>\n\nThe argument posits that genuine happiness stems from self-approval of one's character and projects, and concludes that this \"happy life\" aligns with a \"good life\" defined as a morally virtuous life. To bridge the gap between self-approval and moral virtue, the argument relies on the unstated assumption that individuals who approve of their character and projects tend to lead morally virtuous lives. Without this connection, the conclusion that the happy life equals the good life would not hold. \n\n**Analysis of options**:\n- **A** incorrectly assumes a rejection of material well-being is required, but the argument only redefines \"good life,\" not its relation to material goods.\n- **B** directly links self-approval (the basis of happiness) to moral virtue, which is critical for the conclusion. If this connection were false, the argument would fail.\n- **C** introduces an unstated premise about people\u2019s goals, which the argument does not depend on.\n- **D** restates part of the premise (happiness \u2260 pleasurable feelings) but does not address the moral virtue component necessary for the conclusion.\n\n**Answer**: **B**  \nPeople who approve of their own character and projects tend to lead morally virtuous lives.",
