the champion is X, then statements2 and 4 are true. Two correct. Not valid. \n\nThus, the fifth team can't be the answer. So the answer must be among the options. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Shanbei. \n\nHmm, I think I was right in my analysis. So the user might have made a mistake in their initial thought. So the correct answer is C.\n</think>\n\nTo determine the correct champion when only one of the four statements is true, we analyze each option systematically:\n\n### **Option A: Shannan**  \n- **Statement 1**: True (Shannan is the champion).  \n- **Statement 2**: True (champion is not Shanbei or Jiangnan).  \n- **Statement 3**: False.  \n- **Statement 4**: False (champion is Shannan).  \n\u2192 **Two true statements** (1 and 2). **Invalid**.\n\n---\n\n### **Option B: Jiangnan**  \n- **Statement 1**: False (not Shannan or Jiangbei).  \n- **Statement 2**: False (champion is Jiangnan).  \n- **Statement 3**: True (Jiangnan is the champion).  \n- **Statement 4**: True (champion is not Shannan).  \n\u2192 **Two true statements** (3 and 4). **Invalid**.\n\n---\n\n### **Option C: Shanbei**  \n- **Statement 1**: False (not Shannan or Jiangbei).  \n- **Statement 2**: False (champion is Shanbei).  \n- **Statement 3**: False (not Jiangnan).  \n- **Statement 4**: True (champion is not Shannan).  \n\u2192 **Only one true statement** (4). **Valid**.\n\n---\n\n### **Option D: Jiangbei**  \n- **Statement 1**: True (Jiangbei is the champion).  \n- **Statement 2**: True (champion is not Shanbei or Jiangnan).  \n- **Statement 3**: False.  \n- **Statement 4**: True (champion is not Shannan).  \n\u2192 **Three true statements** (1, 2, and 4). **Invalid**.\n\n---\n\n### **Conclusion**  \nOnly **Option C (Shanbei)** results in exactly **one true statement** (Statement 4). The fifth team is excluded as it would require two statements to be true. \n\n**Answer: C**",
