      "text": " C.\n</think>\n\nThe HR person's conclusion is that it is impossible for **all** candidates to be hired due to limited positions. This logically implies that **some candidates must not be hired** (since \"not all\" means at least one is excluded). \n\n### Analysis of Options:\n- **A)** \"Some candidates may be hired\" is true but does not address the necessity of excluding candidates. It does not directly lead to the conclusion.  \n- **B)** \"There may be candidates who will not be hired\" uses \"may,\" which only expresses possibility, not necessity. The conclusion requires a stronger claim (necessity), so this is insufficient.  \n- **C)** \"There are bound to be candidates who will not be hired\" uses \"bound to be,\" indicating inevitability. This directly aligns with the conclusion that **it is impossible for all candidates to be hired**, as it necessitates at least some rejections.  \n- **D)** \"There must be candidates to be hired\" focuses on the necessity of hiring *some*, which is unrelated to the conclusion about the impossibility of hiring *all*.\n\n### Correct Answer:\n**C)** In this recruitment examination, there are bound to be candidates who will not be hired.  \n\nThe conclusion hinges on the necessity of rejecting some candidates, which matches the phrasing of **Option C**.",
