messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nPrice demand elasticity: refers to the responsiveness or sensitivity of demand to price changes, which is expressed by the absolute value of the coefficient EP = demand change (%)  divided by  price change (%).\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following statements is correct?\n# Options:\nA) When the elasticity coefficient of demand price ep > 1, it means that the change of price will cause a large change in demand.\nB) When ep = 1, it indicates that the price change has little impact on the demand change, so the price can be increased as much as possible.\nC) The size of ep actually reflects the sensitivity of demand to cost changes.\nD) According to the law of supply and demand, there can be no commodity with ep < 1.. I think the correct option is: C\n', '<think>Okay, let\'s tackle this question step by step. First, I need to recall what price demand elasticity means. The context says it\'s the responsiveness of demand to price changes, measured by the absolute value of EP, which is the percentage change in demand divided by the percentage change in price.\n\nLooking at the options:.\n\nOption A: If ep > 1, then demand is elastic. That means a 1% increase in price leads to more than a 1% decrease in demand. So the statement says price changes cause a large change in demand. Since elasticity greater than 1 implies that demand is indeed responsive (large change in demand relative to price), this seems correct. But let me check others.\n\nOption B: When ep = 1, it\'s unit elastic. Price change percentage equals demand change percentage. The statement says price can be increased as much as possible because it has little impact. Wait, no. If ep=1, then increasing price would decrease revenue by the same proportion, so revenue stays the same. But the statement says "little impact on demand change", which isn\'t accurate because the demand does change proportionally. Also, increasing price as much as possible might not be advisable because even though revenue is stable, other factors might matter. So B is probably wrong.\n\nOption C: The question says ep measures sensitivity to price changes, not cost changes. The wording here says "sensitivity of demand to cost changes", which is incorrect. So C is wrong.\n\nOption D: The law of supply and demand doesn\'t state that there can\'t be goods with ep <1. In fact, inelastic demand (ep <1) exists, like necessities. For example, insulin has inelastic demand because people need it regardless of price. So D is incorrect. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
