in D, the conclusion is drawn because if it were unfair (~A), then B would be true. But the problem is that the argument is structured as "since [second premise], therefore ~A." Maybe there\'s a different structure here.\n\nWait, the problem says "since this person would have been likely to win the lottery if it were unfair, the lottery was probably unfair." So the reasoning is: If ~A, then B. Therefore, since B (the person was likely to win), then ~A. But that\'s affirming the consequent, which is a logical fallacy. Wait, but actually, the first premise is A→~B. So if B is true, then ~A must be true (modus tollens). However, in D\'s wording, the second premise is introduced with "since," which might be indicating that the person\'s likelihood to win is because of the unfairness. But the actual structure of the argument is combining both conditionals to conclude ~A. Hmm, this might be different.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the correct answer is C because it\'s a direct modus tollens, whereas D is using an extra premise. The original argument uses only one conditional and the observation of B to conclude ~A. Option C does the same. Option D requires two conditionals and might be using a different form of reasoning.\n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is C. But I\'m confused because sometimes these questions can have nuances. Wait, let me check the options again.\n\nWait, looking back at the problem\'s options, the original\'s conclusion is "highly unlikely that Juan was at the party," which is the negation of A. The same as option C\'s conclusion: "it is quite unlikely that Clarissa missed the bus." So same structure. Therefore, the answer should be C.\n\nBut maybe I made a mistake because I recall that sometimes in these questions, the answer is D. Wait, let me think again. Wait, in the original, the first premise is "If A, then ~B". The second premise is B occurred. Therefore, ~A. So the structure is A→~B, B, therefore ~A.\n\nIn option C: A→~B, B occurred, so ~A