ed.  \n\n**B)**  \n- Uses inductive generalization (most insurance sellers are friendly), not a conditional argument. Structure mismatch.  \n\n**C)**  \n- **Premise**: If Clarissa missed the bus (A), she wouldn\u2019t get to work on time (~B).  \n- **Premise**: She was on time (B).  \n- **Conclusion**: She didn\u2019t miss the bus (~A).  \n\nThis matches the original structure exactly: observing B leads to denying A via modus tollens.  \n\n**D)**  \n- **Premise 1**: If the lottery were fair (A), the winner wouldn\u2019t be likely (~B).  \n- **Premise 2**: If unfair (~A), the winner would be likely (B).  \n- **Conclusion**: The lottery was unfair (~A).  \n\nWhile the first premise mirrors the original, the second premise introduces an additional conditional. The conclusion relies on disjunctive reasoning rather than a direct modus tollens. The original did not require a second conditional.  \n\n### Conclusion:\n**C** most closely mirrors the original argument\u2019s reasoning. The structure\u2014denying the antecedent (A) because the consequent\u2019s negation (B) occurred\u2014is identical.  \n\n**Answer: C**",
