et a full vote, so Li Zilu must vote for all candidates. Wait, that can\'t be, because then Li Zilu\'s vote would include everyone, but maybe that\'s the case.\n\nWait, let me think again. If a candidate was not selected by any voter, then they didn\'t get a full vote. But since "full vote" would mean every voter selected them, but since ZH didn\'t vote for anyone, no candidate can have a full vote. Therefore, all candidates didn\'t get full votes, so Li Zilu must vote for all candidates. But that would mean Li Zilu voted for everyone, but the options involve whether he voted for Wang Ming or not. Hmm, maybe my interpretation is wrong.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps "didn\'t get the full vote" refers to not being selected by every other voter except themselves. Wait, maybe the translation is off. Alternatively, maybe "full vote" means that they received votes from all eligible voters except themselves? That might not make sense. Alternatively, maybe "Li Zilu ticked all those who didn\'t get the full vote from him". But that would be contradictory.\n\nAlternatively, maybe "Li Zilu ticked all those who didn\'t get the full vote" means that Li Zilu voted for all candidates except those who received a full vote from others. But since no one can get a full vote (because Zhang Hao didn\'t vote), then Li Zilu would vote for everyone. But that would make option B possible if they ticked each other, but I need to check.\n\nAlternatively, maybe "didn\'t get the full vote" from Li Zilu himself? Like, if a candidate didn\'t receive Li Zilu\'s vote, then Li Zilu would have to vote for them? That doesn\'t make sense.\n\nHmm, perhaps I need to re-examine the original Chinese to ensure the translation is correct. Since I can\'t do that, I have to work with the given translation. Let me try another angle.\n\nSuppose the candidates are three people, including Zhao Jie, Wang Ming, and Li Zilu. So they are all candidates and voters. Let\'s see:\n\nZhang Hao (a voter) didn\'t vote for anyone