at's exactly what the conclusion is saying. So B is correct.\n\nBut I initially thought D. Maybe I confused the options. Let me check again:\n\nOption D: Very few people have seen detailed maps of sea floor extensions even though such maps can be seen in almost every major library. \n\nIf the maps exist in libraries, then scientists could access them, so their knowledge would be there. But if few people have seen them, maybe including scientists? But the premise says that you need sophisticated instruments to do the study, so the maps are created with those instruments, but once created, scientists can access them. So maybe the premise is that without the instruments, you can't do the study, so the knowledge is limited until you have the instruments. But the conclusion is that scientists know less because they need the instruments. The support would be that indeed, without those instruments, the study couldn't be done, leading to less knowledge. So if the maps exist in libraries, maybe that's the result of the instruments, so the existence shows that once you have the instruments, you can get the data, but the problem is that before the instruments, you couldn't. But the conclusion is that currently, scientists know less than other environments. Maybe the other environments (like land) don't require such instruments, so their knowledge is more advanced. \n\nSo maybe the support is that undersea requires more sophisticated tech, making it harder to study, hence less known. Option B supports that by saying the undersea currents are harder to understand than air currents (which are on land and maybe easier). So B is better. \n\nTherefore, I think the correct answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe argument concludes that scientists know less about the undersea environment than any other environment because studying it requires sophisticated instruments. To strengthen this, the correct option should highlight why undersea study is uniquely challenging compared to terrestrial environments, thereby justifying the knowledge gap.\n\n**Option B** states that ocean floor currents are harder to understand than air currents on land. This directly supports the premise by emphasizing that undersea phenomena require more advanced methods (e.g., sophisticated instruments) than land-based equivalents, which can be studied with simpler tools. This contrast strengthens the conclusion that undersea environments are less understood due to their inherent difficulty and reliance on specialized technology.\n\n**Option D**, while mentioning limited access to sea floor maps, focuses on dissemination rather than the necessity of instruments for data collection. Since the argument hinges on the *requirement* for advanced tools to study the sea floor, not on accessibility of existing maps, this is less relevant to the conclusion.\n\n**Final Answer: B**",
