o re-examine the options again.\n\nWait, the question is about foreign exchange in France. The definition says "used for international settlement". So even if the currency is domestic, if it\'s used for international settlement, does it count? Probably not, because the definition requires the voucher to be in a foreign currency. .\n\nHmm, maybe the key is that the Spanish bonds are foreign currency securities even if in euros? Wait no, because the currency must be foreign. Since Spain\'s bonds are in euros, same as France\'s, they are not foreign currency. So they don\'t qualify. .\n\nWait, perhaps the question is a trick. Let me check the options again:.\n\nOption C is converting rubles (foreign currency) into euros (domestic), so the certificate is in euros. So that\'s not foreign. .\n\nOption A: Spanish bonds in euros are not foreign currency. .\n\nOption B: HSBC\'s certificate. If the CD is in a foreign currency (like USD) then yes. But the problem says "Certificate of deposit with HSBC" without specifying currency. Since the question is in the context of France, maybe HSBC France offers CDs in euros, so that would be domestic. But the question might be considering HSBC as a foreign bank, but the currency is key. Since the question doesn\'t specify, maybe we can\'t assume that. Alternatively, maybe the certificate is in a foreign currency, so it is foreign exchange. .\n\nOption D is converting euros to USD, so the certificate is in USD, which is foreign. So D is foreign exchange. .\n\nSo between A and C as possible answers. Since the question is from an exam, perhaps there\'s a detail I missed. Let me recheck the options:.\n\nWait the question is asking which does NOT belong. So the correct answer must be either A or C. .\n\nWait, maybe in option C, even though it\'s converted to euros, the fact that it was converted from rubles makes it foreign exchange? No, the definition is based on the voucher\'s currency, not the source. The certificate\'s currency is what matters. So if it\'s in euros, then no. .\n\nHmm, maybe the answer is C? Or A?.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the Spanish bonds are considered foreign securities even in euros. Wait the definition says "foreign currency securities", so the currency has to be foreign. So if the security is in euros, it\'s domestic, so not foreign exchange. So A is excluded. .\n\nC is excluded as well, but since the options don\'t have two answers, perhaps I\'m missing something. .\n\nWait, looking at the options again: the question is in French context. .\n\nOption B: Certificate of deposit with HSBC. If HSBC is a foreign bank but the CD is in euros, then it\'s still domestic currency, so not foreign exchange. But if the CD is in another currency, like USD, then yes. Since the question doesn\'t specify, maybe this is a trick. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question considers that the certificate itself is a foreign voucher even if in euros because it\'s from a foreign bank? But the definition requires the voucher to be in foreign currency. .\n\nHmm. I\'m getting confused. Let\'s try to think differently. .\n\nThe options not in foreign currencies (i.e., euros) are A and C. The others are in foreign currencies (B and D, assuming B is in foreign). Since the question asks which does not belong, the answer is either A or C. .\n\nBut the options are A, B, C, D. Since the problem is from an exam, likely only one is correct. Let me think again.\n\nWait the question says "foreign exchange refers to .. foreign currencies". So anything in euros is not foreign exchange in France. So A (Spanish bonds in euros) is not. C (euros after conversion) is not. So which is the answer?.\n\nWait perhaps the question considers that the Spanish bonds are in foreign currency even if Spain uses euros? No, Spain uses euros, so their bonds are in euros. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the Spanish bonds are in another currency? Unlikely, as Spain is in Eurozone. .\n\nHmm. Maybe there\'s a mistake here, but I have to choose be