her. But if the leader said "At least one of L or W," then the Secretary\'s disagreement is neither. .\n\nSince the problem states the class leader\'s statement as "Either Li Xue is rated or Wang Lei is rated," without any exclusion, the standard interpretation is inclusive OR. Therefore, the negation is neither, so answer B. However, I need to confirm. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe in Chinese, the phrasing "either A or B" is exclusive, so the answer would be C. Since the user is presenting a problem that\'s originally in Chinese, perhaps the intended answer is C. .\n\nWait, let me think of an example. Suppose the class leader says, "We\'ll have either pizza or burgers for the party." If someone disagrees, they might mean "We won\'t have either," which is B. But if the leader meant only one, not both, the negation could be both or neither. .\n\nIn formal logic terms, the negation of (A ∨ B) is ¬A ∧ ¬B. So the answer should be B. But I might be overcomplicating. Let me check the options again. .\n\nOption C says "Either both are rated or neither is." That is (L ∧ W) ∨ (¬L ∧ ¬W). .\n\nIf the class leader\'s statement is L ∨ W, then the negation is indeed ¬L ∧ ¬W, which is B. So why is there an option C? .\n\nPerhaps the class leader\'s statement was meant as an exclusive OR. Let me see the wording again. The class leader says: "Either Li Xue is rated as an outstanding graduate or Wang Lei is rated as an outstanding graduate." The use of "either..or.." might imply that only one can be chosen, so it\'s an exclusive OR. Therefore, the negation would be that both are chosen or neither is, which is option C. .\n\nIn that case, the answer is C. But how to decide?.\n\nAnother approach: the Youth League Secretary says "I disagree" with the class leader\'s statement. So the Secretary\'s position must be the logical opposite of the leader\'s statement. .\n\nIf the leader\'s statement is "L or W" (inclusive), the opposite is "Neither," so B. .\n\nIf the leader\'s statement is "Exactly one of L or W" (exclusive OR), then the opposite is "Both or neither," so C. .\n\nSince the problem didn\'t specify "exactly one," but used "either..or..", which can be ambiguous, but in logical reasoning tests, sometimes "either..or.." is considered exclusive. For example, in LSAT or GMAT questions, "either/or" is often treated as exclusive. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe in Chinese context, the phrasing used would be exclusive. .\n\nAlternatively, looking at the options, if B is possible, but C is also a possibility, which is more likely? Let me think of the logical structure again. .\n\nIf the class leader\'s statement is an inclusive OR, then the negation is B. But option C is the negation of an exclusive OR. Since the problem is about accurately expressing the Secretary\'s disagreement, perhaps the question assumes that the leader\'s statement was an exclusive OR. Because if it were inclusive, the Secretary could just be saying neither, but maybe the leader intended only one, so the Secretary is allowing both or neither. .\n\nAlternatively, the problem could be testing the concept of the negation of a disjunction. Let me recall: the negation of "A or B" is "not A and not B". So that\'s B. So unless there\'s a reason to think it\'s exclusive, the answer should be B. .\n\nHowever, sometimes in natural language, "either..or.." is exclusive. For instance, if someone says "You can have either tea or coffee," they usually mean you can\'t have both. So in that case, the negation would be "You can\'t have tea or coffee, or you can have both." Wait, no. If the original was exclusive, then the negation would include the case where both are chosen. .\n\nHmm, this is tricky. Let me see if there\'s another way. .\n\nSuppose the class leader\'s statement is "Either L or W is selected, but not both." Then the negation would be "Either both are selected or neither is selected." That would be option C. .\n\nBut if the leader\'s statement is just "At least one of L or W is selected," then the negation is "Neither is selected," option B. .\n\nThe problem is that the original statement\'s exact meaning isn\'t clear. The key is how the Chinese phrasing works. Since the user provided the translation as "Either Li Xue is rated as an outstanding graduate or Wang Lei is rated as an outstanding graduate," without any exclusivity, it\'s safer to assume it\'s inclusive OR, leading to B. .\n\nHowever, in many standardized tests, when someone uses "either/or" and you have to negate it, the answer often requires considering the exclusive case. Wait, let me think of an example: .\n\nIf a statement is "Either Alice or Bob will go," and someone disagrees, does that mean "Neither will go" or "Both will go or neither will go"? .\n\nIn formal terms, the negation is "Neither will go," but if the original was intended as exclusive, then the negation includes both possibilities. .\n\nSince the problem is from a Chinese context, perhaps the correct answer is C. .\n\nAlternatively, let me think of it as the Youth League Secretary is rejecting the class leader\'s proposal. If the class leader proposed that one of them must be chosen, the Secretary could be saying that neither should be chosen (B), but maybe the Secretary thinks both should be chosen, which would also contradict the leader\'s "either/or." .\n\nWait, the leader\'s statement is that at least one must be chosen (if inclusive OR). The Secretary disagrees, so the Secretary is saying that it\'s not the case that at least one is chosen, meaning neither is chosen (B). .\n\nAlternatively, if the leader\'s proposal was that exactly one is chosen (exclusive OR), then the Secretary could be saying that either both are chosen or neither are, which would be C. .\n\nSince the problem doesn\'t specify whether the leader\'s "either/or" is exclusive, but in many such problems, unless stated, it\'s inclusive. Therefore, B is the answer. .\n\nHowever, I\'m a bit torn because in some cases "either/or" is exclusive. Let me see the options again. Option C includes both being chosen, which would directly contradict an exclusive OR. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is expecting the answer to be C. Let me think of the logical equivalence. The negation of an exclusive OR (XOR) is the biconditional (both or neither). Since the Secretary disagrees with the class leader, if the leader\'s statement was an XOR, then the Secretary\'s position is the biconditional. .\n\nAlternatively, the problem might be testing knowledge of logical negations. Let me recall that the negation of "A or B" is "not A and not B," so B is correct. .\n\nGiven that, I think the answer is B. But maybe I\'m missing something. .\n\nAlternatively, maybe the Youth League Secretary is saying that the class leader\'s statement is false, so the actual situation must be the negation. .\n\nSo if the class leader\'s statement is "L ∨ W," the negation is "¬L ∧ ¬W," which is option B. Therefore, the correct answer is B. .\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is B. But wait, let me check the options again. .\n\nWait, the options are:.\n\nA) Both are rated.\n\nB) Neither can be rated.\n\nC) Either both are rated, or neither is rated.\n\nD) Li is rated, Wang isn\'t.\n\nSo if the leader\'s statement is L∨W, the Secretary\'s negation is B. .\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be B. .\n\nAlternatively, if the leader\'s statement was an exclusive OR, then the negation would be C, but since there\'s no indication of that, I think B is correct. .\n\nSo I\'ll go with B. .\n\nWait, but another way: if the class leader says "Either L or W," and the Secretary disagrees, maybe the Secretary is saying that both can be rated, which would also contradict the leader\'s statement if the leader meant exclusive OR. But the Secretary\'s disagreement could include both possibilities. .\n\nHmm, but the problem asks which option "accurately expresses the Secretary\'s opinion." The Secretary\'s opinion is simply the negation of the leader\'s statement. So if the leader\'s statement is "L or W," then the negation is "not (L or W)" which is "neither," so B. .\n\nTherefore, I think the answer is B. .\n\nWait, but let me think again. Suppose the leader says "Either L or W is selected," and the Secretary says "I disagree." So the Secretary is saying that it\'s not true that at least one of them is selected. Therefore, the only possibility is that neither is selected. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 98%|#########7| 586/600 [3:48:35<05:26, 23.32s/it]
