quinine are in the mouth, and then water tastes sweet. It might be that the combination of salt and quinine in the mouth causes the water to taste sweet, which is a different phenomenon. Maybe it's a case of contrast or some cross-modulation. The definition requires that one substance weakens another's taste. If salt weakens quinine's bitterness, then that's antagonism, but the sweet taste is an additional effect. So the presence of salt reduces quinine's bitterness (antagonism), but the question is whether the scenario as a whole counts as antagonism. Since the main point is that the presence of one substance weakens another's taste, then C could still be an example of antagonism. So the key is whether the scenario describes weakening of a taste.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the sweet taste is an illusion, but the question is whether the antagonism is happening. The problem states that antagonism is when one weakens another's taste. In C, maybe the salt and quinine together are causing a sweet taste, but neither is weakening the other's taste characteristics. For instance, if the salt doesn't make the quinine less bitter, but their combination creates a new taste, then it's not antagonism. So perhaps C is not an example of antagonism.\n\nHmm, this is getting confusing. Let me try to approach each option again:\n\nThe definition: Antagonism is when the presence of one odorous substance weakens the taste of another odorant.\n\nA) Citric acid reduces sweetness (another odorant is sugar?), and sugar reduces sourness (citric acid). So both substances are weakening each other's tastes. So yes, A is antagonist.\n\nB) Sodium glutamate reduces saccharin's bitterness. One substance (glutamate) weakens another's (saccharin's bitterness). Antagonism.\n\nC) After taking salt and quinine, water tastes sweet. Here, the presence of salt and quinine (different substances) leads to a sweet taste. If the salt is weakening the bitterness of quinine, allowing the water's taste (maybe water 